window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-chem-thermodynamics","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Thermodynamics · Practice","chapter":"Thermodynamics","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Use the following data at 298 K:\nStandard enthalpy of combustion of $C_2H_5OH(l)$ = $-1367$ kJ mol$^{-1}$\n$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of $CO_2(g)$ = $-393.5$ kJ mol$^{-1}$\n$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of $H_2O(l)$ = $-285.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$\nCombustion: $C_2H_5OH(l) + 3O_2(g) \\rightarrow 2CO_2(g) + 3H_2O(l)$\nFind the internal energy change $\\Delta_f U^\\circ$ for forming 1 mol of liquid ethanol from its elements in their reference states. (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["-277.4 kJ mol^-1","-268.7 kJ mol^-1","-286.1 kJ mol^-1","-271.2 kJ mol^-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1 (Hess's law): $\\Delta_c H = [2(-393.5) + 3(-285.8)] - \\Delta_f H(C_2H_5OH)$.\nSo $\\Delta_f H = -787 - 857.4 + 1367 = -277.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nStep 2: The formation reaction is $2C(graphite) + 3H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}O_2(g) \\rightarrow C_2H_5OH(l)$. Hence $\\Delta n_g = 0 - 3.5 = -3.5$.\nStep 3: $\\Delta U = \\Delta H - \\Delta n_g RT = -277.4 + 3.5(8.314\\times10^{-3})(298) = -277.4 + 8.67$.\nSo $\\Delta_f U \\approx -268.7$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The standard enthalpy of formation of CO(g) from graphite and oxygen cannot be measured directly by calorimetry.\nReason (R): When graphite is burnt to form CO, some CO$_2$ is always formed as well.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the enthalpy change for C(graphite) + $\\frac{1}{2}$O$_2$ → CO cannot be measured directly.\nR is true. Although CO is the major product, some CO$_2$ is always produced.\nR is the correct explanation of A. The measured heat would include a contribution from CO$_2$ formation, so the value is instead obtained by Hess's law (−393.5 + 283.0 = −110.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match the systems in List-I with their correct description in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Reactants kept in an open beaker\n(b) Reactants kept in a sealed vessel made of copper\n(c) Reactants kept in a thermos flask\nList-II\n(i) Energy can be exchanged with the surroundings, but matter cannot\n(ii) Neither energy nor matter can be exchanged with the surroundings\n(iii) Both energy and matter can be exchanged with the surroundings","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) An open beaker is an open system. Matter, such as vapours, and energy can both cross its imaginary boundary, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) A sealed copper vessel is a closed system. Copper conducts heat, so energy can be exchanged, but no matter can enter or leave. So (b)-(i).\n(c) A thermos flask is an insulated closed vessel. Neither heat nor matter crosses its boundary, so it is an isolated system: (c)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The dissolution of NaCl(s) in water is accompanied by very little heat change, because its lattice enthalpy ($+788$ kJ mol$^{-1}$) is almost cancelled by its hydration enthalpy ($-784$ kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nStatement II: For most ionic compounds $\\Delta_{sol}H^\\ominus$ is negative, so their solubility in water decreases with rise in temperature.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $\\Delta_{sol}H = \\Delta_{lattice}H + \\Delta_{hyd}H = 788 - 784 = +4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$, which is a very small heat change.\nStatement II is incorrect. For most ionic compounds $\\Delta_{sol}H$ is positive, meaning dissolution is endothermic. That is why their solubility increases with rise in temperature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas kept in a thermos flask (adiabatic walls) is compressed. 250 J of work is done on the gas. What is the change in internal energy of the gas?","image":"","options":["+250 J","$-250$ J","0 J","+500 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: adiabatic walls, so $q = 0$; work done on the system, $w$ = +250 J.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = q + w = w_{ad}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U = 0 + 250$ = +250 J.\nWork done on the system is positive, so the internal energy rises. It is not zero, because only $q$ is zero.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas initially occupies 1 L at 10 atm. It is expanded isothermally to 10 L in two irreversible steps:\n(i) against a constant external pressure of 2 atm, until the volume is 5 L;\n(ii) then against a constant external pressure of 1 atm, until the volume is 10 L.\nWhat is the total heat absorbed by the gas, in L atm?","image":"","options":["9.0 L atm","13.0 L atm","23.03 L atm","18.0 L atm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the process is isothermal, so $\\Delta U = 0$ and $q = -w$.\nFormula: for multistep work, $w = -\\Sigma\\, p_{ex}\\Delta V$.\nStep (i): $2 \\times (5 - 1) = 8$ L atm. Step (ii): $1 \\times (10 - 5) = 5$ L atm.\nSo $w = -13$ L atm and q = 13.0 L atm.\nFor comparison, a single step against 1 atm gives 9 L atm and the reversible path gives 23.03 L atm. Splitting the expansion into more steps increases the work done by the gas.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Combustion reactions are exothermic, so $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ is negative.\n(ii) $\\Delta_a H$ of CH$_4$ equals 4 × 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$, where 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$ is the enthalpy for CH$_4$ → CH$_3$ + H.\n(iii) The mean C–H bond enthalpy in CH$_4$ is about 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\n(iv) $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ of graphite is numerically equal to $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of CO$_2$(g).\n(v) When a thermochemical equation is reversed, $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ keeps the same sign.\n(vi) Complete combustion of 2 mol butane ($\\Delta_c H^\\circ = -2658$ kJ mol$^{-1}$) releases 5316 kJ of heat.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT states that combustion reactions are exothermic.\n(ii) Incorrect: the four successive steps differ (427, 439, 452, 347), and their sum is 1665, not 1708.\n(iii) Correct: 1665/4 ≈ 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\n(iv) Correct: C(graphite) + O$_2$ → CO$_2$ is both the combustion of graphite and the formation of CO$_2$.\n(v) Incorrect: the sign of $\\Delta_r H$ is reversed.\n(vi) Correct: 2 × 2658 = 5316 kJ.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a reaction at 298 K, $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = +5.70$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the approximate value of its equilibrium constant? ($R = 8.314$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["0.10","10","1.0","0.01"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = 5700$ J mol$^{-1}$, T = 298 K.\nFormula: $\\log K = -\\frac{\\Delta_rG^\\circ}{2.303RT}$.\nSubstituting: $\\log K = -\\frac{5700}{2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 298} = -\\frac{5700}{5706} \\approx -1.00$.\nSo K = antilog(−1.00) ≈ 0.10. This is less than 1, as expected for a positive $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) For a given change of state, $q$ and $w$ individually depend on the path, but $q + w$ does not.\n(ii) Heat absorbed at constant pressure, $q_p$, is independent of path.\n(iii) The absolute value of the internal energy of a system in a given state can be measured, just like its volume.\n(iv) If a system is heated from 25°C to 35°C, either directly or after first cooling it by a few degrees, the change in temperature is +10°C in both cases.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. $q + w = \\Delta U$, and $U$ is a state function.\n(ii) Correct. $q_p = \\Delta H$, and $H = U + pV$ is a state function.\n(iii) Incorrect. Unlike volume, only changes in internal energy, $\\Delta U$, can be measured, not its absolute value.\n(iv) Correct. $T$ is a state function, so $\\Delta T$ is independent of the route taken.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the isothermal compression of an ideal gas between the same initial and final volumes, the work done on the gas is less in a reversible compression than in a single-step compression against a constant $p_{ex}$ equal to the final pressure.\nReason (R): In a reversible compression, the external pressure at each stage is only infinitesimally greater than the pressure of the gas.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In the single-step case, the work equals the rectangle $p_{ex}(V_i - V_f)$ drawn at the highest (final) pressure. In the reversible case, the work equals the smaller area under the p–V curve.\nR is true. In reversible compression, $p_{ex} = p_{in} + dp$ at every stage.\nR explains A: because $p_{ex}$ follows the gas pressure, the area under the curve is the minimum possible.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about enthalpy is correct?","image":"","options":["For an exothermic reaction carried out at constant pressure, both $q_p$ and $\\Delta H$ are negative","For a reaction involving gases at constant pressure, the heat absorbed equals $\\Delta U$","Enthalpy is a path-dependent quantity because heat $q$ depends on the path","For an endothermic reaction, $\\Delta H$ is negative because heat enters the system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: at constant pressure $\\Delta H = q_p$. In an exothermic reaction the system loses heat, so both are negative.\n(2) Wrong: heat absorbed at constant pressure equals $\\Delta H$. Heat absorbed at constant volume equals $\\Delta U$.\n(3) Wrong: $H = U + pV$ is built from state functions, so H is itself a state function. That is why $q_p$ becomes path independent.\n(4) Wrong: when heat is absorbed, $\\Delta H$ is positive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Gibbs energy G is an extensive property and a state function.\n(b) $\\Delta G$ has the units of energy, because $T\\Delta S$ has units of K × J K$^{-1}$ = J.\n(c) At constant temperature and pressure, a process with $\\Delta G < 0$ is spontaneous.\n(d) A spontaneous reaction must proceed at a measurable rate once the reactants are mixed.\n(e) A strongly endothermic reaction with a large positive $\\Delta_rH$ is generally expected to have K much greater than 1.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: NCERT states that G is an extensive property and a state function.\n(b) Correct: both $\\Delta H$ and $T\\Delta S$ are energy terms.\n(c) Correct: this is the spontaneity criterion at constant T and p.\n(d) Incorrect: spontaneity says nothing about rate. A mixture of H$_2$ and O$_2$ can stand for years without visible change.\n(e) Incorrect: such reactions generally have K much smaller than 1.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about internal energy (U) is correct according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["The absolute value of U of a system in a given state can be measured, just like its volume","Only the change in internal energy, $\\Delta U$, can be measured; the absolute value of U cannot be specified","U depends on the path by which a state is reached","U includes only the kinetic energy of the molecules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT contrasts energy with a mechanical property such as volume. Volume has an unambiguous absolute value in a given state, but U does not.\nWe can measure only $\\Delta U$ between two states.\nU is a state function, so (3) is wrong.\nU is the sum of all forms of energy (chemical, electrical, mechanical, etc.), so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Because water has a large heat capacity, a given amount of heat produces only a small rise in its temperature.\nStatement II: If the amount of a substance is doubled, its molar heat capacity also doubles.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: from $q = C\\Delta T$, a large C gives a small $\\Delta T$ for a given q.\nStatement II is incorrect: heat capacity C is directly proportional to the amount of substance, so C doubles. However, the molar heat capacity $C_m = C/n$ stays the same because it is an intensive property.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are accompanied by an increase in entropy of the system?\n(i) A liquid crystallises into a solid\n(ii) A crystalline solid is heated from 0 K to 115 K\n(iii) $2NaHCO_3(s) \\rightarrow Na_2CO_3(s) + CO_2(g) + H_2O(g)$\n(iv) $H_2(g) \\rightarrow 2H(g)$\n(v) $2Cl(g) \\rightarrow Cl_2(g)$\n(vi) Sublimation of dry ice\n(vii) Condensation of steam to water","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Entropy decreases: the solid is more ordered than the liquid.\n(ii) Entropy increases: the particles begin to oscillate about their lattice positions.\n(iii) Entropy increases: a solid reactant gives two gases.\n(iv) Entropy increases: the number of particles doubles.\n(v) Entropy decreases: two atoms combine into one molecule.\n(vi) Entropy increases: solid changes to gas.\n(vii) Entropy decreases: gas changes to liquid.\nIncreases: (ii), (iii), (iv), (vi), which is 4 processes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For glucose, $\\Delta_c H^\\circ = -2802.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ (products CO$_2$(g) and H$_2$O(l)). Given $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$[CO$_2$(g)] = $-393.5$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$[H$_2$O(l)] = $-285.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$, what is $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of glucose, C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$(s)?","image":"","options":["$-1273.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+1273.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+155.2$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-6877.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$ + 6O$_2$ → 6CO$_2$ + 6H$_2$O, $\\Delta_c H = -2802.0$ kJ.\nFormula: $\\Delta_c H$ = [6$\\Delta_f H$(CO$_2$) + 6$\\Delta_f H$(H$_2$O)] − $\\Delta_f H$(glucose). The $\\Delta_f H$ of O$_2$ is zero.\nSubstituting: $-2802.0 = (-2361.0 - 1714.8) - \\Delta_f H$(glucose).\nSo $\\Delta_f H$(glucose) = $-4075.8 + 2802.0 = -1273.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Given at 298 K: $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$($Fe_2O_3$, s) = –824.2 kJ $mol^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$($H_2O$, l) = –285.83 kJ $mol^{-1}$. What is $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ for the reaction written as\n$\\frac{1}{3}Fe_2O_3(s) + H_2(g) \\rightarrow \\frac{2}{3}Fe(s) + H_2O(l)$?","image":"","options":["–11.10 kJ $mol^{-1}$","–33.30 kJ $mol^{-1}$","+11.10 kJ $mol^{-1}$","–16.60 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ values above. $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ = 0 for Fe(s) and $H_2(g)$ because they are elements in their reference states.\nFormula: $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus = \\sum a_i \\Delta_f H^\\ominus(\\text{products}) - \\sum b_i \\Delta_f H^\\ominus(\\text{reactants})$\nSubstituting: $1(-285.83) - \\frac{1}{3}(-824.2) = -285.83 + 274.73$\nSo $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ = –11.10 kJ $mol^{-1}$. This is one-third of –33.3 kJ $mol^{-1}$ (the value for the equation with 1 mol $Fe_2O_3$), because enthalpy is extensive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Heat absorbed by a system at constant volume\n(B) Heat absorbed by a system at constant pressure\n(C) $C_p - C_V$ for one mole of an ideal gas\n(D) Heat absorbed in terms of heat capacity and temperature rise\nList-II\n(i) R\n(ii) $\\Delta H$\n(iii) $\\Delta U$\n(iv) $C\\Delta T$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) At constant volume no work is done, so $\\Delta U = q_V$ → (iii).\n(B) At constant pressure, $q_p = \\Delta H$ → (ii).\n(C) For an ideal gas, $C_p - C_V = R$ → (i).\n(D) Heat absorbed is $q = C\\Delta T$ → (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas with $C_V = \\frac{3}{2}R$ is heated at constant pressure from 300 K to 400 K. How much heat is absorbed by the gas? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["2.49 kJ","4.16 kJ","1.66 kJ","5.82 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: n = 2 mol, $\\Delta T$ = 100 K, $C_V = \\frac{3}{2}R$.\nFormula: $C_p = C_V + R = \\frac{5}{2}R$, and at constant pressure $q_p = \\Delta H = nC_p\\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: $q_p$ = 2 × 2.5 × 8.314 × 100 = 4157 J.\nSo q ≈ 4.16 kJ.\nThe distractor 2.49 kJ is $nC_V\\Delta T$, which equals $\\Delta U$. The distractor 1.66 kJ is the expansion work $nR\\Delta T$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Use the following data to calculate the lattice enthalpy of KCl(s), defined as $KCl(s) \\rightarrow K^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$:\n$\\Delta_fH^\\ominus$[KCl(s)] = −437 kJ mol$^{-1}$; $\\Delta_{sub}H$(K) = 89 kJ mol$^{-1}$; $\\Delta_iH$(K) = 419 kJ mol$^{-1}$; bond dissociation enthalpy of $Cl_2$ = 242 kJ mol$^{-1}$; $\\Delta_{eg}H$(Cl) = −349 kJ mol$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["+717 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−157 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+838 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+1415 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Born–Haber cycle (Hess's law): start from KCl(s), go back to the elements, then form the gaseous ions.\nFormula: $\\Delta_{lattice}H = -\\Delta_fH + \\Delta_{sub}H + \\Delta_iH + \\frac{1}{2}BE(Cl_2) + \\Delta_{eg}H$.\nSubstituting: 437 + 89 + 419 + 121 − 349 = +717 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nUsing the full 242 instead of half gives 838. Using the wrong sign of $\\Delta_fH$ gives −157.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (enthalpy term).\nList-I\n(P) $Na(s) \\rightarrow Na(g)$\n(Q) $Na(g) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + e^-(g)$\n(R) $Na^+Cl^-(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$\n(S) $HCl\\cdot 25\\,aq + 15\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl\\cdot 40\\,aq$\nList-II\n(1) Lattice enthalpy\n(2) Enthalpy of dilution\n(3) Ionization enthalpy\n(4) Enthalpy of sublimation","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1","P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) A solid changing directly to its vapour is sublimation, so P–4.\n(Q) Removing an electron from a gaseous atom is ionization enthalpy, so Q–3.\n(R) One mole of an ionic solid separating into gaseous ions is the lattice enthalpy, so R–1.\n(S) Adding more solvent to an existing solution is dilution, so S–2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the reaction $CaO(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s)$, $\\Delta_r H^\\circ = -178.3$ kJ mol$^{-1}$, but this value is not the standard enthalpy of formation of $CaCO_3(s)$.\nReason (R): A standard enthalpy of formation refers to forming one mole of a compound from its constituent elements in their reference states.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In this reaction $CaCO_3$ is formed from two compounds, CaO and $CO_2$, not from Ca, C and $O_2$.\nR is true. $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ is the enthalpy change for forming 1 mol of a compound from its elements in their most stable states.\nR explains A, because the reactants in A are compounds, not elements in their reference states.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A sample is burnt completely in a bomb calorimeter whose heat capacity is 15.0 kJ K$^{-1}$. The temperature of the calorimeter rises by 0.80 K. What is $\\Delta U$ for the combustion of the sample?","image":"","options":["$-12.0$ kJ","$+12.0$ kJ","$-18.75$ kJ","$-1.2$ kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $C_V = 15.0$ kJ K$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta T = 0.80$ K. The volume is constant, so $\\Delta U = q_V$.\nFormula: heat gained by the calorimeter is $C_V\\Delta T$. The reaction loses the same amount, so $q = -C_V\\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: $q = -15.0 \\times 0.80 = -12.0$ kJ.\nSo $\\Delta U = -12.0$ kJ. The negative sign shows the combustion is exothermic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The heat change measured in a bomb calorimeter equals $\\Delta U$ of the reaction, even when gaseous products are formed.\nReason (R): The steel bomb is sealed, so the reaction occurs at constant volume and no pressure-volume work is done.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In a bomb calorimeter the heat exchanged at constant volume is $q_V$, and $\\Delta U = q_V$.\nR is true. The sealed steel bomb does not change volume, so $\\Delta V = 0$ and $w = -p_{ex}\\Delta V = 0$. This holds even when gases are produced.\nWith $w = 0$, the first law $\\Delta U = q + w$ gives $\\Delta U = q_V$. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (standard enthalpy change in kJ $mol^{-1}$).\nList-I:\n(a) $H_2O(s) \\rightarrow H_2O(l)$ at 273 K\n(b) $H_2O(l) \\rightarrow H_2O(g)$ at its boiling point\n(c) $CO_2(s) \\rightarrow CO_2(g)$ at 195 K\n(d) Naphthalene(s) → naphthalene(g)\nList-II:\n(i) 73.0\n(ii) 25.2\n(iii) 6.00\n(iv) 40.79","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Fusion of ice: $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\ominus$ = 6.00 kJ/mol, so (iii).\n(b) Vaporisation of water at its boiling point: $\\Delta_{vap}H^\\ominus$ = 40.79 kJ/mol, so (iv).\n(c) Dry ice sublimes at 195 K with $\\Delta_{sub}H$ = 25.2 kJ/mol, so (ii).\n(d) Naphthalene sublimes slowly with $\\Delta_{sub}H$ = 73.0 kJ/mol, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Ionization enthalpy of a gaseous atom at temperature T exceeds its ionization energy (defined at 0 K) by $\\frac{5}{2}RT$","For $NaCl(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$, the internal energy change is smaller than the lattice enthalpy by 2RT","The mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane equals the enthalpy change of $CH_4(g) \\rightarrow CH_3(g) + H(g)$","For a diatomic molecule such as $Cl_2$, the enthalpy of atomization equals its bond dissociation enthalpy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. For ionization, $\\Delta_r C_p = +\\frac{5}{2}R$, so $\\Delta_i H = E_0 + \\frac{5}{2}RT$.\n(2) Correct. $\\Delta n_g$ = +2, so $\\Delta U = \\Delta H - 2RT$, which gives 783 kJ instead of 788 kJ.\n(3) Incorrect. The first C–H dissociation needs 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The mean value is $\\frac{1}{4}$ × 1665 = 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$, because each successive step needs a different energy.\n(4) Correct. Atomizing a diatomic molecule breaks exactly one bond.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Using mean bond enthalpies H–H = 435.8, Cl–Cl = 243 and H–Cl = 431 kJ mol$^{-1}$, estimate $\\Delta_rH$ for the gas-phase reaction $H_2(g) + Cl_2(g) \\rightarrow 2HCl(g)$.","image":"","options":["−183.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+183.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+247.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−1540.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula (gas-phase reactions only): $\\Delta_rH$ = Σ BE(reactants) − Σ BE(products).\nBonds broken: 435.8 + 243 = 678.8 kJ.\nBonds formed: 2 × 431 = 862 kJ.\nSubstituting: 678.8 − 862 = −183.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$, so the reaction is exothermic.\nForgetting the coefficient 2 for HCl gives +247.8. Reversing the subtraction gives +183.2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["For isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, $\\Delta U = 0$ for both reversible and irreversible paths, but $\\Delta S_{total}$ is non-zero only for the irreversible path","The entropy of a supercooled liquid becomes zero as the temperature approaches 0 K","For $NaCl(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$, the lattice internal energy change is smaller than the lattice enthalpy by $2RT$","A reaction can be spontaneous even if it proceeds so slowly that no change is noticed for years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $\\Delta U$ does not distinguish reversible from irreversible processes, but $\\Delta S_{total}$ does.\n(2) Incorrect: the third law applies only to pure crystalline solids. NCERT notes that solutions and supercooled liquids have non-zero entropy at 0 K.\n(3) Correct: $\\Delta n_g = 2$, so $\\Delta U = \\Delta H - 2RT$, which gives about 783 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\n(4) Correct: spontaneity says nothing about rate. $H_2$ and $O_2$ mixed at room temperature are an example.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (change in internal energy).\nList-I:\n(A) Work w is done on the system; no heat is absorbed\n(B) No work is done; heat q is taken out of the system\n(C) Work w is done by the system; heat q is supplied to it\n(D) No heat or work is exchanged\nList-II:\n(P) $\\Delta U = q - w$\n(Q) $\\Delta U = 0$\n(R) $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$\n(S) $\\Delta U = -q$","image":"","options":["A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q","A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q","A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q","A-P, B-S, C-R, D-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) No heat transfer means an adiabatic wall, so $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$, which is R.\n(B) Heat leaves through conducting walls and w = 0, so $\\Delta U = -q$, which is S.\n(C) Heat in is +q and work done by the system is −w, so $\\Delta U = q - w$, which is P.\n(D) An isolated system has $\\Delta U = 0$, which is Q.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the standard enthalpy change at 298 K for the reaction\n$\\frac{1}{2}Fe_2O_3(s) + \\frac{3}{2}H_2(g) \\rightarrow Fe(s) + \\frac{3}{2}H_2O(g)$\nGiven: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ values (kJ $mol^{-1}$): $H_2O(l)$ = –285.83, $Fe_2O_3(s)$ = –824.2; $\\Delta_{vap}H^\\circ$ of water at 298 K = 44.01 kJ $mol^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["–16.6 kJ $mol^{-1}$","+98.7 kJ $mol^{-1}$","–82.7 kJ $mol^{-1}$","+49.4 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"First find $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of steam: liquid water formation plus vaporisation gives –285.83 + 44.01 = –241.82 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\n$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of Fe(s) and $H_2$(g) is zero, since both are in their reference states.\nSo $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ = 1.5(–241.82) – 0.5(–824.2) = –362.73 + 412.10 = +49.4 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nOption (1) is the value for liquid water as product. Option (2) is for the full (unhalved) equation. Option (3) subtracts $\\Delta_{vap}H$ instead of adding it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"1 mol of an ideal gas at 300 K expands isothermally from 10 atm to 1 atm in two different ways: (a) in a single step against a constant external pressure of 1 atm, and (b) reversibly. What is the ratio of the heat absorbed in (a) to the heat absorbed in (b)? (log 10 = 1)","image":"","options":["0.391","2.56","0.900","0.434"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $V_i = RT/10$ and $V_f = RT/1$, so $V_f/V_i = 10$.\n(a) $q_a = p_{ex}(V_f - V_i) = 1 \\times (RT - 0.1RT) = 0.9RT$.\n(b) $q_b = 2.303RT\\log 10 = 2.303RT$.\nRatio = 0.9/2.303 = 0.391. The irreversible path absorbs less heat because it does less work.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of ideal gas occupying 2 L at 10 atm and 298 K expands isothermally to a final volume of 10 L. Three paths are considered: (a) into vacuum, (b) against a constant 1 atm, (c) reversibly. What is the heat absorbed by the gas along path (c), in L atm? (log 5 = 0.6990)","image":"","options":["8.0 L atm","13.98 L atm","32.19 L atm","46.06 L atm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $nRT = p_iV_i = 10 \\times 2 = 20$ L atm, and $V_f/V_i = 5$.\nFormula: for reversible isothermal expansion, $q = -w = 2.303\\,nRT\\log\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$.\nSubstituting: $q = 2.303 \\times 20 \\times 0.6990 = 32.19$ L atm.\nCompare the other paths: path (a) gives q = 0 and path (b) gives $1 \\times 8 = 8$ L atm. The reversible path absorbs the most heat.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Heat capacity, C, of a sample is an extensive property.\n(ii) Molar heat capacity, $C_m$, is independent of the amount of substance.\n(iii) For a given amount of heat supplied, a body with a large heat capacity shows a small rise in temperature.\n(iv) The relation $C_p - C_V = R$ holds for one mole of any liquid.\n(v) For an ideal gas, $C_p > C_V$ because at constant pressure part of the heat supplied is used for expansion work.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: C is directly proportional to the amount of substance, so it is extensive.\n(ii) Correct: $C_m = C/n$ is a molar property and does not depend on the amount.\n(iii) Correct: from $q = C\\Delta T$, a large C means a small $\\Delta T$ for the same q.\n(iv) Incorrect: $C_p - C_V = R$ is derived for an ideal gas using $\\Delta(pV) = R\\Delta T$. It does not apply to liquids.\n(v) Correct: at constant pressure, some heat goes into expansion work, so more heat is needed per kelvin.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (name of the enthalpy change):\nList-I\n(A) $H_2(g) \\rightarrow 2H(g)$\n(B) $Na(s) \\rightarrow Na(g)$\n(C) $Na^+Cl^-(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$\n(D) $Cl(g) + e^- \\rightarrow Cl^-(g)$\nList-II\n(I) Lattice enthalpy\n(II) Electron gain enthalpy\n(III) Bond dissociation enthalpy\n(IV) Enthalpy of sublimation\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Breaking one mole of H–H bonds into gaseous atoms is the bond dissociation enthalpy (III). For a diatomic molecule this equals its atomization enthalpy.\n(B) Converting solid sodium directly into its vapour is sublimation (IV).\n(C) One mole of an ionic solid dissociating into its gaseous ions is the lattice enthalpy (I).\n(D) A gaseous atom gaining an electron gives the electron gain enthalpy (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The energies needed to break the four C–H bonds of $CH_4$ one after another are equal, because all four bonds are identical.\n(ii) The lattice enthalpy of NaCl cannot be measured directly, so it is found from a Born–Haber cycle using Hess's law.\n(iii) For $NaCl(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$, the internal energy change is smaller than the lattice enthalpy by 2RT.\n(iv) The enthalpy of solution of HCl(g) becomes less negative as more water is used for dissolving it.\n(v) The relation $\\Delta_rH$ = Σ BE(reactants) − Σ BE(products) is exact even for reactions involving liquids.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the successive values are 427, 439, 452 and 347 kJ mol$^{-1}$, so they are not equal.\n(ii) Correct: lattice enthalpy is obtained indirectly from the Born–Haber cycle.\n(iii) Correct: $\\Delta n_g$ = +2, so $\\Delta U = \\Delta H - 2RT$ (788 → 783 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\n(iv) Incorrect: the values go −69.01 → −72.03 → −74.85 kJ mol$^{-1}$, i.e. more negative.\n(v) Incorrect: the relation is approximate and valid only for all-gaseous systems.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A combustible substance is burnt in a sealed steel bomb that is immersed in a water bath. The heat evolved, calculated from the temperature rise, gives directly which quantity for the reaction?","image":"","options":["Enthalpy change, $\\Delta H$","Internal energy change, $\\Delta U$","Gibbs energy change, $\\Delta G$","Pressure–volume work, $w$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The bomb is sealed, so the reaction occurs at constant volume.\nWith $\\Delta V = 0$, $w = 0$, and the first law gives $\\Delta U = q_V$.\nSo a bomb calorimeter measures $\\Delta U$.\n$\\Delta H$ equals $q_p$, the heat at constant pressure, so the bomb does not measure $\\Delta H$ directly.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Given:\n$HCl(g) + 10\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl\\cdot 10\\,aq$; $\\Delta H$ = −69.01 kJ mol$^{-1}$\n$HCl(g) + 25\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl\\cdot 25\\,aq$; $\\Delta H$ = −72.03 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nWhat is the enthalpy change for $HCl\\cdot 10\\,aq + 15\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl\\cdot 25\\,aq$?","image":"","options":["−3.02 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+3.02 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−0.76 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−141.04 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Subtract the first equation from the second (Hess's law). This gives $HCl\\cdot 10\\,aq + 15\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl\\cdot 25\\,aq$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H$ = −72.03 − (−69.01) = −3.02 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThis is an enthalpy of dilution. Its value depends on the starting concentration and the amount of solvent added.\nThe value −0.76 kJ mol$^{-1}$ belongs to the 25 aq → 40 aq dilution, not this one.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a diatomic molecule such as $Cl_2$, the enthalpy of atomization is equal to its bond dissociation enthalpy.\nStatement II: For $CH_4$, the enthalpy of atomization equals four times the enthalpy needed to break the first C–H bond, $CH_4(g) \\rightarrow CH_3(g) + H(g)$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $Cl_2(g) \\rightarrow 2Cl(g)$ breaks one bond and gives only gaseous atoms, so atomization and bond dissociation are the same process.\nStatement II is incorrect. The successive C–H dissociation enthalpies are different: 427, 439, 452 and 347 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Their sum is 1665 kJ mol$^{-1}$, not $4 \\times 427$ = 1708 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Given: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of CO(g) = $-110.5$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ of C(graphite) = $-393.5$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the standard enthalpy change for $\\text{CO(g)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{O}_2\\text{(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2\\text{(g)}$?","image":"","options":["$-283.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-504.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+283.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-110.5$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: C + O$_2$ → CO$_2$, $\\Delta H = -393.5$ kJ; C + ½O$_2$ → CO, $\\Delta H = -110.5$ kJ.\nFormula (Hess's law): subtract the second equation from the first, which gives CO + ½O$_2$ → CO$_2$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H = -393.5 - (-110.5)$.\nSo $\\Delta H = -283.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are state functions?\nInternal energy, pressure, heat absorbed, work done, temperature, volume","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Internal energy: a state function (shown by Joule's experiments).\nPressure, temperature and volume: state functions whose values depend only on the state. NCERT gives the volume of water in a pond as an example.\nHeat absorbed and work done: path dependent, so not state functions.\nThat gives 4 state functions in total.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the heat required to decompose 25 g of $CaCO_3(s)$ into $CaO(s)$ and $CO_2(g)$ under standard conditions.\nGiven: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ values (kJ $mol^{-1}$): $CaCO_3(s)$ = –1206.9, $CaO(s)$ = –635.1, $CO_2(g)$ = –393.5; molar mass of $CaCO_3$ = 100 g $mol^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["89.2 kJ","178.3 kJ","44.6 kJ","713.2 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 25 g $CaCO_3$, M = 100 g $mol^{-1}$, so n = 0.25 mol.\nFormula: $\\Delta_r H^\\circ = \\sum \\Delta_f H^\\circ(products) - \\sum \\Delta_f H^\\circ(reactants)$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ = (–635.1 – 393.5) – (–1206.9) = +178.3 kJ $mol^{-1}$ (endothermic).\nFor 0.25 mol: q = 0.25 × 178.3 = 44.6 kJ.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Bomb calorimeter\n(B) Calorimeter open to the atmosphere\n(C) $C(graphite) + O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g)$\n(D) $H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}O_2(g) \\rightarrow H_2O(l)$\nList-II\n(p) $\\Delta_r H = q_p$\n(q) $\\Delta U = q_V$\n(r) $\\Delta H < \\Delta U$\n(s) $\\Delta H = \\Delta U$","image":"","options":["A-q, B-p, C-s, D-r","A-p, B-q, C-s, D-r","A-q, B-p, C-r, D-s","A-p, B-q, C-r, D-s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The bomb is sealed, so the measurement is at constant volume and $\\Delta U = q_V$: q.\n(B) A calorimeter open to the atmosphere works at constant pressure, so $\\Delta_r H = q_p$: p.\n(C) For graphite combustion, $\\Delta n_g = 1 - 1 = 0$, so $\\Delta H = \\Delta U$: s.\n(D) For hydrogen combustion to liquid water, $\\Delta n_g = 0 - 1.5 = -1.5$. Then $\\Delta H = \\Delta U - 1.5RT$, so $\\Delta H < \\Delta U$: r.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following sets contains only intensive properties?","image":"","options":["Volume, pressure, temperature","Enthalpy, molar enthalpy, density","Temperature, density, molar volume","Heat capacity, specific heat, temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An intensive property does not depend on the amount of matter. An extensive property does.\nTemperature and density are intensive. Molar volume $V_m = V/n$ is an extensive property divided by the amount, so it is also intensive.\nVolume, enthalpy and heat capacity are extensive. Each of the other three sets contains one of them, so those sets fail.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For NaCl, the following data (kJ/mol) are given:\n$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$[NaCl(s)] = $-411.2$\n$\\Delta_{sub} H^\\circ$[Na] = $108.4$\n$\\Delta_i H^\\circ$[Na] = $496$\nBond dissociation enthalpy of $Cl_2$ = $242$\n$\\Delta_{eg} H^\\circ$[Cl] = $-348.6$\nUsing a Born–Haber cycle, the lattice enthalpy of NaCl(s) is:","image":"","options":["$+788$ kJ/mol","$+909$ kJ/mol","$-788$ kJ/mol","$+1485.2$ kJ/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lattice enthalpy refers to $NaCl(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$.\nPath via the elements: reverse formation ($+411.2$), sublimation ($+108.4$), $\\frac{1}{2}Cl_2 \\rightarrow Cl$ ($+\\frac{1}{2}\\times 242 = +121$), ionization ($+496$), electron gain ($-348.6$).\nSum: $411.2 + 108.4 + 121 + 496 - 348.6 = +788$ kJ/mol.\nUsing the full 242 kJ instead of half gives 909 kJ/mol, which is wrong because only half a mole of $Cl_2$ is needed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The standard enthalpy of fusion of every solid is positive.\nReason (R): Melting of a solid requires absorption of heat to overcome the attractive forces holding the particles in the solid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that all enthalpies of fusion are positive. For ice, $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\ominus$ = +6.00 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nR is true. Melting is endothermic because heat is absorbed to loosen the particles.\nR explains A. Because heat is absorbed during melting, $\\Delta H$ for fusion must be positive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For a reaction at equilibrium at constant T and p, $\\Delta_r G = 0$.\n(ii) If $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ is negative, the equilibrium constant K is greater than 1.\n(iii) Gibbs energy G is an intensive property.\n(iv) A reaction with $\\Delta H < 0$ and $\\Delta S < 0$ becomes non-spontaneous at sufficiently high temperature.\n(v) A spontaneous reaction must take place rapidly once the reactants are mixed.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the criterion for equilibrium is $\\Delta_r G = 0$.\n(ii) Correct: from $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$, a negative $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ gives $\\ln K > 0$, so $K > 1$.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT states that G is an extensive property and a state function.\n(iv) Correct: at high T, $-T\\Delta S$ is large and positive, so $\\Delta G$ becomes positive.\n(v) Incorrect: a mixture of H$_2$ and O$_2$ reacts spontaneously but extremely slowly. Spontaneity says nothing about rate.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Using mean bond enthalpies, estimate $\\Delta_r H$ for the gas-phase reaction\n$CH_4(g) + Cl_2(g) \\rightarrow CH_3Cl(g) + HCl(g)$\nGiven mean bond enthalpies (kJ/mol): C–H = 414, Cl–Cl = 243, C–Cl = 330, H–Cl = 431.","image":"","options":["$-104$ kJ/mol","$+104$ kJ/mol","$-346$ kJ/mol","$+17$ kJ/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $\\Delta_r H = \\Sigma$ BE(reactants) $- \\Sigma$ BE(products).\nOnly one C–H bond and one Cl–Cl bond are broken. One C–Cl bond and one H–Cl bond are formed. The other three C–H bonds cancel.\nBonds broken: $414 + 243 = 657$ kJ/mol.\nBonds formed: $330 + 431 = 761$ kJ/mol.\nSo $\\Delta_r H = 657 - 761 = -104$ kJ/mol, an exothermic reaction. Reversing the order of subtraction gives the wrong sign, $+104$ kJ/mol.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are accompanied by an increase in the entropy of the system?\n(a) Sublimation of naphthalene\n(b) H$_2$O(g) → H$_2$O(l)\n(c) CaCO$_3$(s) → CaO(s) + CO$_2$(g)\n(d) Raising the temperature of a crystal from 0 K to 50 K\n(e) Diffusion of two gases into each other after a partition is removed\n(f) N$_2$(g) + 3H$_2$(g) → 2NH$_3$(g)","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Increase: solid → vapour.\n(b) Decrease: gas → liquid.\n(c) Increase: a gas is produced from a solid.\n(d) Increase: particles start vibrating about their lattice positions.\n(e) Increase: the mixed system is more chaotic and less predictable.\n(f) Decrease: 4 mol of gas form 2 mol of gas.\nSo 4 processes show an increase in entropy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas expands isothermally and reversibly at 300 K from 5 L to 50 L. How much heat does the gas absorb? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, log 10 = 1)","image":"","options":["$-11.49$ kJ","$4.99$ kJ","$11.49$ kJ","$0$ kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: n = 2 mol, T = 300 K, $V_f/V_i$ = 10.\nFormula: for an isothermal reversible change, $\\Delta U = 0$, so $q = -w = 2.303\\,nRT\\log\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$.\nSubstituting: $q = 2.303 \\times 2 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times 1 = 11488$ J.\nSo q = +11.49 kJ, absorbed by the gas. The value $-11.49$ kJ is the work done, not the heat.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following processes does the entropy of the system decrease?","image":"","options":["Sublimation of solid CO$_2$ (dry ice)","Condensation of steam into liquid water","Dissociation of Cl$_2$(g) into 2Cl(g)","Heating a crystalline solid from 0 K to 50 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Solid changes to gas, so disorder increases and $\\Delta S > 0$.\n(2) Gas changes to liquid, so the molecules become more ordered and $\\Delta S < 0$.\n(3) One molecule gives two atoms, so the number of particles increases and $\\Delta S > 0$.\n(4) Particles that are static at 0 K start to vibrate when heated, so $\\Delta S > 0$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of 50.0 g of water is heated from 25 °C to 45 °C. The specific heat of water is 4.18 J g$^{-1}$ °C$^{-1}$. How much heat is absorbed by the water?","image":"","options":["4.18 kJ","2.09 kJ","8.36 kJ","0.209 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 50.0 g, c = 4.18 J g$^{-1}$ °C$^{-1}$, $\\Delta T$ = 45 − 25 = 20 °C.\nFormula: $q = c \\times m \\times \\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: q = 4.18 × 50.0 × 20 = 4180 J.\nSo the heat absorbed = 4.18 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In a single-step compression at constant $p_{ex}$, the work done on the gas equals the rectangular area $p_{ex}(V_i - V_f)$ on the p–V plot.\n(ii) If the pressure changes in a finite number of steps, the work done on the gas is $-\\Sigma p\\Delta V$.\n(iii) Work is a state function, so it is the same for every path between two states.\n(iv) For the isothermal reversible expansion of an ideal gas, $q = -w$.\n(v) Free expansion of an ideal gas involves work only when it is carried out reversibly.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the shaded rectangle under $p_{ex}$ represents the work.\n(ii) Correct: the work is summed over all the steps.\n(iii) Incorrect: w is path dependent; only $q + w = \\Delta U$ is independent of path.\n(iv) Correct: $\\Delta U = 0$ for an isothermal ideal gas, so $q = -w$.\n(v) Incorrect: free expansion involves zero work whether reversible or irreversible.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A mixture of dihydrogen and dioxygen at room temperature can be kept for many years without any noticeable formation of water. Yet the reaction $H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}O_2(g) \\rightarrow H_2O(l)$ is called spontaneous. Which statement best explains this?","image":"","options":["Spontaneity means the process has the potential to proceed without help from an external agency; it says nothing about the rate","A spontaneous reaction must start as soon as the reactants are brought into contact","The reaction is spontaneous only because it is exothermic, and every exothermic reaction is fast","The reaction is spontaneous only in the reverse direction, so water slowly decomposes instead"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines spontaneity as 'having the potential to proceed without the assistance of an external agency'.\nThis definition gives no information about how fast the change happens. The $H_2$ + $O_2$ reaction is spontaneous but extremely slow at room temperature.\nOption (2) is the common misconception that spontaneous means immediate.\nOption (3) fails because exothermicity does not decide the rate.\nOption (4) fails because water does not decompose into $H_2$ and $O_2$ on its own.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Two ideal gases in an isolated container diffuse into each other when the partition between them is removed. This happens even though $\\Delta H = 0$.\nStatement II: In an isolated system $\\Delta U = 0$, so the direction of spontaneous change is decided by an increase in entropy.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT uses the diffusion of gases A and B as an example of a spontaneous process with no change in enthalpy. The system simply becomes more disordered.\nStatement II is correct. The energy of an isolated system stays constant, so energy cannot decide the direction of change. An increase in entropy ($\\Delta S > 0$) does, and this is the essence of the second law.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The standard enthalpy of vaporisation of water at 373 K is 40.79 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Assuming water vapour behaves as an ideal gas, what is the internal energy change when 36 g of liquid water is vaporised at 373 K and 1 bar? (H = 1, O = 16; R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["75.38 kJ","81.58 kJ","37.69 kJ","87.78 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 36/18 = 2 mol. For $H_2O(l) \\rightarrow H_2O(g)$, $\\Delta n_g$ = +1 per mol.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = \\Delta H - \\Delta n_g RT$ per mole.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_{vap}U$ = 40.79 − (8.314 × 373 × $10^{-3}$) = 40.79 − 3.10 = 37.69 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFor 2 mol: 2 × 37.69 = 75.38 kJ.\nThe distractor 37.69 kJ is the value for 1 mol only. The distractor 87.78 kJ comes from adding RT instead of subtracting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (standard enthalpy change in kJ $mol^{-1}$, as given in NCERT):\nList-I\n(A) Fusion of ice at 273 K\n(B) Vaporisation of water at its boiling point\n(C) Sublimation of dry ice at 195 K\n(D) Sublimation of naphthalene\nList-II\n(I) 73.0\n(II) 25.2\n(III) 40.79\n(IV) 6.00","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Melting of ice: $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\circ$ = 6.00 kJ $mol^{-1}$, so A-IV.\n(B) Vaporisation of water at $T_b$: $\\Delta_{vap}H^\\circ$ = 40.79 kJ $mol^{-1}$, so B-III.\n(C) Dry ice (solid $CO_2$) sublimes at 195 K: $\\Delta_{sub}H^\\circ$ = 25.2 kJ $mol^{-1}$, so C-II.\n(D) Naphthalene sublimes slowly: $\\Delta_{sub}H^\\circ$ = 73.0 kJ $mol^{-1}$, so D-I.\nAll four values are positive because each change absorbs heat.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a system in which no energy is exchanged with the surroundings either as heat or as work, the change in internal energy is:","image":"","options":["equal to q","equal to w","zero","always negative"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Such a system is an isolated system, so q = 0 and w = 0.\nFrom $\\Delta U = q + w$, we get $\\Delta U$ = 0.\nThis is the statement of the first law: the energy of an isolated system is constant.\nOptions (1) and (2) would also give zero here, but they do not express the general result. U cannot fall without some energy leaving, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Ice melts reversibly at its normal melting point, 273 K. The enthalpy of fusion of ice is 6.00 kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the entropy change when 1 mol of ice melts?","image":"","options":["21.98 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$","2.198 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$","219.8 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$","45.5 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $q_{rev}$ = 6.00 kJ mol$^{-1}$ = 6000 J mol$^{-1}$, T = 273 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta S = \\frac{q_{rev}}{T}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta S = \\frac{6000}{273}$\nSo $\\Delta S$ = 21.98 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. The value is positive because the liquid is more disordered than the solid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (relation that applies).\nList-I:\n(a) Adiabatic process\n(b) Process at constant volume\n(c) Isothermal reversible expansion of an ideal gas\n(d) Process at constant pressure\nList-II:\n(i) $\\Delta H = q_p$\n(ii) $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$\n(iii) $q = -w = 2.303\\,nRT\\log\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$\n(iv) $\\Delta U = q_V$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Adiabatic process: q = 0, so $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$, which is (ii).\n(b) Constant volume: no pressure-volume work is done, so $\\Delta U = q_V$, which is (iv).\n(c) Isothermal process for an ideal gas: $\\Delta U = 0$, so $q = -w = 2.303\\,nRT\\log\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$, which is (iii).\n(d) Constant pressure: heat absorbed equals the enthalpy change, $\\Delta H = q_p$, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For the complete combustion of butane gas to $CO_2(g)$ and $H_2O(l)$, the heat released per mole is greater when the reaction is carried out at constant pressure than in a bomb calorimeter.\nStatement II: At constant pressure, the gaseous volume decreases in this reaction. The surroundings therefore do work on the system, and this extra energy must also leave as heat.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For $C_4H_{10}(g) + \\frac{13}{2}O_2(g) \\rightarrow 4CO_2(g) + 5H_2O(l)$, $\\Delta n_g = 4 - 7.5 = -3.5$.\n$\\Delta H = \\Delta U + \\Delta n_g RT = \\Delta U - 3.5RT$. So $\\Delta H$ is more negative than $\\Delta U$, and $|q_p| > |q_V|$. Statement I is correct.\nThe volume shrinks, so $w = -p\\Delta V > 0$ (work is done on the system). Since $\\Delta U = q + w$ is fixed, $q$ must be more negative. Statement II is correct and explains Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For H$_2$(g), the enthalpy of atomization (435.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$) is the same as its H–H bond dissociation enthalpy.\nStatement II: For Na(s) → Na(g), the enthalpy of atomization (108.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$) is the same as the enthalpy of sublimation of sodium.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in a diatomic molecule there is only one bond. Breaking it gives the gaseous atoms, so $\\Delta_a H$ equals the bond dissociation enthalpy.\nStatement II is correct: converting solid Na into gaseous Na atoms is sublimation, so $\\Delta_a H = \\Delta_{sub} H = 108.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The standard state of solid iron at 500 K is pure iron at 1 bar.\nStatement II: Acetone has a larger molar enthalpy of vaporisation than water because dipole–dipole interactions between acetone molecules are stronger than hydrogen bonds between water molecules.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar. NCERT gives solid iron at 500 K as an example.\nStatement II is incorrect. Water molecules are held by strong hydrogen bonds, while acetone has weaker dipole–dipole interactions. So less heat is needed to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than 1 mol of water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following phase changes is accompanied by a negative enthalpy change?","image":"","options":["Sublimation of dry ice at 195 K","Melting of ice at 273 K","Freezing of liquid water at 273 K","Vaporisation of acetone at its boiling point"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Melting, vaporisation and sublimation all need heat to overcome intermolecular attractions. They are endothermic, so their $\\Delta H$ is positive.\nFreezing is the reverse of fusion. The same amount of heat is given off to the surroundings, so $\\Delta H = -\\Delta_{fus}H < 0$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a reaction at equilibrium at constant temperature and pressure, $\\Delta_rG = 0$.\nReason (R): At equilibrium, the Gibbs energy of the system is at its minimum, so no further spontaneous change can lower it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives the criterion for equilibrium as $\\Delta_rG = 0$.\nR is true. At equilibrium the free energy of the system is minimum. If it were not, the system would change spontaneously to a configuration of lower free energy.\nR explains A: since G cannot decrease further, $\\Delta G$ for any change is zero.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the decomposition $CaCO_3(s) \\rightarrow CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$, take $\\Delta_r H^\\circ = +178.3$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S^\\circ = +160$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Assume both are independent of temperature. Above what temperature does the decomposition become spontaneous under standard conditions?","image":"","options":["1114 K","1.11 K","841 K","1387 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H$ = +178300 J mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta S$ = +160 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Both are positive, so the reaction becomes spontaneous at high T.\nFormula: spontaneity requires $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0$, i.e. $T > \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S}$.\nSubstituting: T = 178300/160 = 1114 K.\nThe 1.11 K option comes from not converting kJ to J.\nSo T must exceed about 1114 K.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A reaction has $\\Delta H = +36.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta S = +120$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Assume both are independent of temperature. Above what temperature does the reaction become spontaneous?","image":"","options":["300 K","0.30 K","3000 K","333 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H = 36000$ J mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta S = 120$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: the reaction becomes spontaneous when $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0$. The crossover temperature is $T = \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S}$.\nSubstituting: $T = \\frac{36000}{120} = 300$ K.\nForgetting to convert kJ to J gives the wrong value 0.30 K.\nSo the reaction is spontaneous above 300 K.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a gas is compressed in a single step by a constant external pressure, the work done on the gas has a positive sign.\nReason (R): In compression $(V_f - V_i)$ is negative, and the work is given by $w = -p_{ex}(V_f - V_i)$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: under the IUPAC convention, work done on the system is positive, and it raises the internal energy.\nR is true: $w = -p_{ex}\\Delta V$, and $\\Delta V < 0$ during compression.\nA negative sign multiplied by a negative $\\Delta V$ gives a positive $w$. R therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the melting of ice at 273 K and 1 bar, $\\Delta H$ and $\\Delta U$ are almost equal.\nReason (R): Solids and liquids do not undergo any significant volume change, so the $p\\Delta V$ term is negligible.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: melting ice is a liquid–solid change with no gases involved, so $\\Delta H \\approx \\Delta U$.\nR is true: NCERT notes that solids and liquids suffer no significant volume change.\nR explains A: since $\\Delta H = \\Delta U + p\\Delta V$ and $\\Delta V \\approx 0$, the two quantities become nearly equal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For which of the following reactions is the standard reaction enthalpy equal to the standard enthalpy of formation of the product?","image":"","options":["$CaO(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s)$","$H_2(g) + Br_2(l) \\rightarrow 2HBr(g)$","$\\frac{1}{2}H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}Br_2(g) \\rightarrow HBr(g)$","$\\frac{1}{2}H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}Br_2(l) \\rightarrow HBr(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ refers to forming exactly 1 mol of a compound from its elements in their reference states.\n(1) Fails: $CaCO_3$ is formed from compounds, not from elements.\n(2) Fails: it forms 2 mol HBr, so $\\Delta_r H^\\circ = 2\\Delta_f H^\\circ$.\n(3) Fails: bromine's reference state is the liquid, not the gas.\n(4) Fits: 1 mol HBr is formed from $H_2$(g) and $Br_2$(l), both in their reference states.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For which of the following forms is the standard enthalpy of formation, $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$, taken as zero at 298 K and 1 bar?","image":"","options":["C(diamond)","S(monoclinic)","C(graphite)","$O_3$(g)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"By convention, $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ is zero only for an element in its reference state. The reference state is its most stable state of aggregation at 25°C and 1 bar.\nThe reference state of carbon is graphite, so $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of C(graphite) is zero.\nDiamond and monoclinic sulphur are less stable allotropes, and the reference state of sulphur is rhombic. Ozone is not the reference form of oxygen, which is $O_2$ gas. All three therefore have non-zero values.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a given change of state, the values of q and w individually depend on how the change is carried out, but their sum (q + w) does not.\nReason (R): q + w equals $\\Delta U$, and internal energy is a state function.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: q and w are path functions, so they vary with the route taken between two states.\nR is true: by the first law, $q + w = \\Delta U$, and U is a state function.\nBecause $\\Delta U$ depends only on the initial and final states, the sum q + w must be path independent. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under the IUPAC convention used in chemistry, the first law is written as $\\Delta U = q + w$, where w is positive when work is done on the system.\nStatement II: Under the older convention still followed in many physics books, work done by the system is taken as positive, so the first law takes the form $\\Delta U = q - w$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In the IUPAC convention, q and w are positive when energy is added to the system, so $\\Delta U = q + w$.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT notes that the earlier convention made work done by the system positive, and physics books still use it.\nWith that sign choice, energy leaves the system as work, so the same law reads $\\Delta U = q - w$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The equilibrium constant of a reaction at 300 K is 100. What is the standard Gibbs energy change for the reaction? ($R = 8.314$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["−11.49 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+11.49 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−4.99 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−5.74 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: K = 100, T = 300 K, so $\\log K = 2$.\nFormula: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -2.303RT\\log K$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times 2 = -11488$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nThe negative sign fits K > 1. Leaving out the factor 2.303 gives −4.99 kJ.\nSo $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ = −11.49 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Although all four C–H bonds in methane are identical in bond length and energy, the enthalpies needed to break them one after another ($CH_4 \\rightarrow CH_3$, $CH_3 \\rightarrow CH_2$, etc.) are different.\nStatement II: The mean C–H bond enthalpy obtained from methane, $\\frac{1}{4}\\Delta_a H^\\circ = 416$ kJ/mol, has exactly the same value in every compound containing a C–H bond, such as $CH_3CH_2Cl$ and $CH_3NO_2$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The successive C–H dissociation enthalpies in $CH_4$ are 427, 439, 452 and 347 kJ/mol. They differ because each fragment left behind is a different species.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that mean C–H bond enthalpies differ slightly from compound to compound, though not by a great deal. So 416 kJ/mol is not an exact universal value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas is taken from state X to state Y by doing 2.5 kJ of work on it inside adiabatic walls. The same gas is then taken from X to Y by a different path, inside thermally conducting walls, during which 1.0 kJ of heat is released to the surroundings. How much work is done on the gas along the second path?","image":"","options":["1.5 kJ","2.5 kJ","3.5 kJ","−1.5 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: path 1 is adiabatic, so $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$ = +2.5 kJ.\nSince U is a state function, $\\Delta U$ for X→Y is still +2.5 kJ along path 2.\nFormula: $w = \\Delta U - q$, with q = −1.0 kJ because heat is released.\nSubstituting: w = 2.5 − (−1.0) kJ\nSo w = +3.5 kJ of work is done on the gas.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A closed system is taken from state A to state B along path I. During this path it absorbs 500 J of heat and does 200 J of work on the surroundings. It is then returned from B to A along path II, during which it releases 400 J of heat to the surroundings. Using the IUPAC convention (positive w = work done on the system), what is the work w for path II?","image":"","options":["+100 J","−100 J","+700 J","−700 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: for path I, q = +500 J and w = −200 J. For path II, q = −400 J.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = q + w$. U is a state function, so $\\Delta U_{B\\to A} = -\\Delta U_{A\\to B}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U_{A\\to B} = 500 - 200 = +300$ J, so $\\Delta U_{B\\to A} = -300$ J.\nFor path II: $w = \\Delta U - q = -300 - (-400) = +100$ J.\nSo 100 J of work is done on the system in path II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"At 400 K and a total pressure of 1 atm, the gas $X_2$ is 20% dissociated according to $X_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2X(g)$. Calculate the standard Gibbs energy change for the dissociation at this temperature. ($R = 8.314$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$, $\\log 2 = 0.301$, $\\log 3 = 0.477$)","image":"","options":["+5.96 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−5.96 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+5.35 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+10.57 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: degree of dissociation α = 0.2, so the total moles are 1 + α = 1.2.\nPartial pressures: $p_{X_2} = \\frac{0.8}{1.2}$ atm and $p_X = \\frac{0.4}{1.2}$ atm.\nFormula: $K_p = \\frac{p_X^2}{p_{X_2}} = \\frac{0.16}{1.2 \\times 0.8} = \\frac{1}{6}$.\n$\\log K_p = -(0.301 + 0.477) = -0.778$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta G^\\circ = -2.303RT\\log K_p = -2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 400 \\times (-0.778) \\approx +5960$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta G^\\circ$ ≈ +5.96 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane from these data: $\\Delta_fH^\\ominus[CH_4(g)]$ = −74.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$; enthalpy of atomization of C(graphite) to C(g) = 716.7 kJ mol$^{-1}$; bond dissociation enthalpy of $H_2$ = 435.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["415.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$","378.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$","633.7 kJ mol$^{-1}$","1663.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Target reaction: $CH_4(g) \\rightarrow C(g) + 4H(g)$. Build it with Hess's law.\nStep 1: reverse the formation of $CH_4$: +74.8 kJ.\nStep 2: atomize carbon: +716.7 kJ. Break $2H_2$ into 4H: 2 × 435.8 = +871.6 kJ.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_aH$ = 74.8 + 716.7 + 871.6 = 1663.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nMean C–H bond enthalpy = 1663.1/4 = 415.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$, close to NCERT's 416.\nUsing +74.8 with the wrong sign gives 378.4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"0.36 g of graphite is burnt completely to $CO_2(g)$ in excess oxygen in a bomb calorimeter at 298 K. The heat capacity of the calorimeter is 20.0 kJ $K^{-1}$, and the temperature rises by 0.59 K. What is the enthalpy of combustion of graphite in kJ $mol^{-1}$? (Atomic mass C = 12 g $mol^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$-393$ kJ $mol^{-1}$","$-11.8$ kJ $mol^{-1}$","$-32.8$ kJ $mol^{-1}$","$+393$ kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $C_V = 20.0$ kJ $K^{-1}$, $\\Delta T = 0.59$ K, mass = 0.36 g.\nHeat from the reaction: $q = -20.0 \\times 0.59 = -11.8$ kJ. This is $\\Delta U$ for 0.36 g.\nPer mole: $\\Delta U = -11.8 \\times \\frac{12}{0.36} = -393$ kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nFor $C(s) + O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g)$, $\\Delta n_g = 0$, so $\\Delta H = \\Delta U = -393$ kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nOption (2) is the heat for the sample only, and option (3) is the value per gram.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Standard enthalpies of combustion at 298 K are: C$_2$H$_4$(g) = $-1411$ kJ mol$^{-1}$, C$_2$H$_6$(g) = $-1560$ kJ mol$^{-1}$, H$_2$(g) = $-285.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ (water formed as liquid). Using Hess's law, find $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ for C$_2$H$_4$(g) + H$_2$(g) → C$_2$H$_6$(g).","image":"","options":["$-136.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+136.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-434.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-3256.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the combustion equations of C$_2$H$_4$ and H$_2$ written forward, and that of C$_2$H$_6$ reversed. Adding them gives the required reaction, since CO$_2$, H$_2$O and O$_2$ cancel.\nFormula: $\\Delta_r H$ = Σ$\\Delta_c H$(reactants) − Σ$\\Delta_c H$(products).\nSubstituting: $(-1411 - 285.8) - (-1560) = -1696.8 + 1560$.\nSo $\\Delta_r H = -136.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Complete combustion of butane (C$_4$H$_{10}$) has $\\Delta_c H^\\circ = -2658$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. How much heat is released when 29 g of butane burns completely? (Molar mass of butane = 58 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["1329 kJ","2658 kJ","664.5 kJ","5316 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass = 29 g, M = 58 g mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta_c H^\\circ = -2658$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: heat released = n × |$\\Delta_c H$|, where n = m/M.\nSubstituting: n = 29/58 = 0.5 mol, so heat = 0.5 × 2658.\nSo heat released = 1329 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Reactants in an open beaker\n(B) Reactants in a closed copper vessel\n(C) Reactants in a thermos flask\n(D) Water in an insulated beaker being churned by paddles\nList-II\n(i) Matter cannot cross the boundary but energy can\n(ii) Both matter and energy can cross the boundary\n(iii) Neither matter nor energy can cross the boundary\n(iv) No heat crosses the boundary, but work is done on the system","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Match each system with the exchange it allows:\n- A: An open beaker is an open system. Matter and energy are both exchanged, so A-(ii).\n- B: A closed conducting copper vessel is a closed system. Energy passes but matter does not, so B-(i).\n- C: A thermos flask is an isolated system. Nothing is exchanged, so C-(iii).\n- D: The insulated beaker has an adiabatic wall. No heat flows, yet the paddles do work on the water (Joule's experiment), so D-(iv).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A system completes a cycle A → B → C → A.\nA → B: adiabatic, 800 J of work is done on the system.\nB → C: constant volume, 300 J of heat is released by the system.\nC → A: the system absorbs 150 J of heat.\nWhat is the work w (IUPAC convention) for the step C → A?","image":"","options":["−650 J","+650 J","−350 J","+350 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: U is a state function, so over the full cycle $\\Delta U_{total}$ = 0.\nA→B: $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$ = +800 J.\nB→C: w = 0, so $\\Delta U = q$ = −300 J.\nSo $\\Delta U_{CA}$ = 0 − 800 + 300 = −500 J.\nFormula: $w = \\Delta U - q$ = −500 − 150 = −650 J.\nSo the system does 650 J of work on the surroundings.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The standard enthalpy of combustion of liquid ethanol at 298 K is −1367 kJ mol$^{-1}$:\nC$_2$H$_5$OH(l) + 3O$_2$(g) → 2CO$_2$(g) + 3H$_2$O(l)\nGiven $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$(CO$_2$, g) = −393.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$(H$_2$O, l) = −285.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the standard enthalpy of formation of C$_2$H$_5$OH(l)?","image":"","options":["−277.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+277.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+294.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−3011.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ = −1367; $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of CO$_2$ = −393.5 and of H$_2$O = −285.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$. $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of O$_2$ = 0.\nFormula: $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ = [2$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$(CO$_2$) + 3$\\Delta_f H^\\circ$(H$_2$O)] − $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$(ethanol).\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$(ethanol) = 2(−393.5) + 3(−285.8) − (−1367) = −787.0 − 857.4 + 1367.\nSo $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ = −277.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nOption (3) results from forgetting the coefficient 3 for water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (signs of $\\Delta_rH$ and $\\Delta_rS$) with List-II (spontaneity behaviour).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\Delta_rH < 0$, $\\Delta_rS > 0$\n(B) $\\Delta_rH < 0$, $\\Delta_rS < 0$\n(C) $\\Delta_rH > 0$, $\\Delta_rS > 0$\n(D) $\\Delta_rH > 0$, $\\Delta_rS < 0$\nList-II:\n(i) Non-spontaneous at all temperatures\n(ii) Spontaneous at all temperatures\n(iii) Spontaneous only at high temperature\n(iv) Spontaneous only at low temperature","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$ for each pair.\n(A) Both terms make $\\Delta G$ negative, so it is negative at all T → (ii).\n(B) $\\Delta H$ is favourable but $-T\\Delta S$ is positive, so $\\Delta G$ is negative only at low T → (iv).\n(C) Here $-T\\Delta S$ must outweigh a positive $\\Delta H$, so it works only at high T → (iii).\n(D) Both terms make $\\Delta G$ positive, so it is positive at all T → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The third law of thermodynamics states that entropy approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero. This statement applies to:","image":"","options":["solutions","supercooled liquids","pure crystalline substances","all substances, whatever their physical state"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Third law: the entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as T approaches 0 K, because a perfect crystal is perfectly ordered at absolute zero.\nTheory and experiment both show that solutions and supercooled liquids have non-zero entropy at 0 K. So options (1), (2) and (4) are wrong.\nThe importance of the law is that it lets us calculate absolute entropies from thermal data alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match the signs of $\\Delta_r H$ and $\\Delta_r S$ in List-I with the behaviour of the reaction in List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) $\\Delta_r H < 0$, $\\Delta_r S > 0$\n(b) $\\Delta_r H < 0$, $\\Delta_r S < 0$\n(c) $\\Delta_r H > 0$, $\\Delta_r S > 0$\n(d) $\\Delta_r H > 0$, $\\Delta_r S < 0$\nList-II:\n(i) Spontaneous only at high temperature\n(ii) Non-spontaneous at all temperatures\n(iii) Spontaneous at all temperatures\n(iv) Spontaneous only at low temperature","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$.\n(a) Both terms make $\\Delta G$ negative, so it is spontaneous at all T, which is (iii).\n(b) The $-T\\Delta S$ term is positive. $\\Delta G$ stays negative only while T is small, so it is spontaneous at low T, which is (iv).\n(c) $T\\Delta S$ must outweigh $\\Delta H$, so it is spontaneous at high T, which is (i).\n(d) Both terms make $\\Delta G$ positive, so it is never spontaneous, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a gaseous atom M, the ionization energy is $E_0 = 500.0$ kJ/mol and the electron affinity is $A = 300.0$ kJ/mol. Both quantities are defined at absolute zero.\nUse the NCERT relations between enthalpy and energy for ionization and electron gain, with $C_p = \\frac{5}{2}R$ for each gaseous species and $R = 8.314$ J/mol K.\nWhat is the enthalpy change at 298 K for $2M(g) \\rightarrow M^+(g) + M^-(g)$?","image":"","options":["$200.0$ kJ/mol","$212.4$ kJ/mol","$187.6$ kJ/mol","$206.2$ kJ/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The process is $M \\rightarrow M^+ + e^-$ followed by $M + e^- \\rightarrow M^-$.\nIonization enthalpy: $\\Delta_i H = E_0 + \\frac{5}{2}RT$.\nElectron gain enthalpy: $\\Delta_{eg} H = -A - \\frac{5}{2}RT$.\nAdding the two: $\\Delta H = E_0 - A$, because the $\\frac{5}{2}RT$ terms cancel.\nSubstituting: $500.0 - 300.0 = 200.0$ kJ/mol.\nAdding the 6.2 kJ correction only once gives the tempting wrong value of 206.2 kJ/mol.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the combustion $C_3H_8(g) + 5O_2(g) \\rightarrow 3CO_2(g) + 4H_2O(l)$ at 298 K, what is the value of $(\\Delta H - \\Delta U)$? Take R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["$-7.43$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+7.43$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-2.48$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-9.91$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: T = 298 K. Liquid water is not counted when finding the change in gaseous moles.\nFormula: $\\Delta H - \\Delta U = \\Delta n_g RT$.\n$\\Delta n_g = 3 - (1 + 5) = -3$.\nSubstituting: $-3 \\times 8.314 \\times 298 = -7433$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta H - \\Delta U = -7.43$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. The option $-9.91$ comes from wrongly counting the $H_2O$ as gas.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II (values in kJ mol$^{-1}$ at 298 K).\nList-I\n(A) H$_2$(g) → 2H(g)\n(B) CH$_4$(g) → C(g) + 4H(g)\n(C) Na(s) → Na(g)\n(D) Mean C–H bond enthalpy in CH$_4$\nList-II\n(i) 108.4\n(ii) 416\n(iii) 435\n(iv) 1665","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The atomization of H$_2$ equals the H–H bond dissociation enthalpy, 435 → (iii).\n(B) Breaking all four C–H bonds of methane gives $\\Delta_a H^\\circ$ = 1665 → (iv).\n(C) Na(s) → Na(g) is sublimation, 108.4 → (i).\n(D) Mean C–H bond enthalpy = $\\frac{1665}{4}$ ≈ 416 → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two litres of an ideal gas at 10 atm and 25 °C expand isothermally to a final volume of 10 litres. Consider the following statements:\n(i) If the expansion is into vacuum, both q and w are zero.\n(ii) If the expansion is against a constant external pressure of 1 atm, the heat absorbed is 8 L atm.\n(iii) If the expansion is carried out reversibly, the work done by the gas is greater in magnitude than in case (ii).\n(iv) In the irreversible expansion against 1 atm, $\\Delta U$ of the gas is non-zero.\n(v) For the free expansion, $\\Delta S_{total}$ is zero because $\\Delta U = 0$.\nHow many of these statements are correct? (log 5 = 0.699)","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $p_{ex} = 0$ gives $w = 0$. Since $\\Delta U = 0$ for an isothermal ideal gas, q = 0 too.\n(ii) Correct: $q = -w = p_{ex}\\Delta V = 1 \\times 8 = 8$ L atm.\n(iii) Correct: $nRT = p_iV_i = 20$ L atm, so $|w_{rev}| = 2.303 \\times 20 \\times \\log 5 \\approx 32.2$ L atm, which is more than 8.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\Delta U = 0$ for any isothermal ideal-gas change.\n(v) Incorrect: free expansion is irreversible, so $\\Delta S_{total} > 0$. $\\Delta U$ does not reveal irreversibility, but $\\Delta S$ does.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the gas-phase reaction $2X(g) \\rightarrow Y(g) + 3Z(g)$ at 300 K, $\\Delta U = +14.0$ kJ and $\\Delta S = +60.0\\ \\text{J K}^{-1}$. Treat all gases as ideal and take $R = 8.314\\ \\text{J K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}$. What is $\\Delta G$, and is the reaction spontaneous at this temperature?","image":"","options":["+0.99 kJ; non-spontaneous","-4.00 kJ; spontaneous","-8.99 kJ; spontaneous","+4.99 kJ; non-spontaneous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta U = 14000$ J, $\\Delta S = 60.0$ J K$^{-1}$, T = 300 K, and $\\Delta n_g = (1+3) - 2 = +2$.\nFormula: $\\Delta H = \\Delta U + \\Delta n_g RT$, then $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H = 14000 + 2(8.314)(300) = 18988$ J.\n$T\\Delta S = 300 \\times 60.0 = 18000$ J.\n$\\Delta G = 18988 - 18000 = +988$ J ≈ +0.99 kJ.\nSince $\\Delta G > 0$, the reaction is non-spontaneous. If you skip the $\\Delta n_g RT$ correction, you get the wrong value of -4.00 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"By convention, the standard enthalpy of formation is taken as zero for an element in its reference state. For which of the following species, at 298 K and 1 bar, is $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ NOT zero?","image":"","options":["$O_2(g)$","C(graphite, s)","S(rhombic, s)","C(diamond, s)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The reference state of an element is its most stable state of aggregation at 25 °C and 1 bar.\nNCERT lists these reference states: dihydrogen as $H_2(g)$, dioxygen as $O_2(g)$, carbon as graphite and sulphur as rhombic sulphur. So $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ = 0 for $O_2(g)$, graphite and rhombic S.\nDiamond is an allotrope of carbon, but it is not the reference state. Forming diamond from graphite involves an enthalpy change, so its $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus \\neq 0$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a reaction, $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ = +40.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S^\\ominus$ = +100 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Assume both are independent of temperature. What is the equilibrium constant K of the reaction at 500 K? (R = 8.314 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$; take $e^{2.41} \\approx 11.1$)","image":"","options":["11.1","0.090","2.41","1.10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H^\\ominus$ = 40 000 J mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta S^\\ominus$ = 100 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$, T = 500 K.\nStep 1: $\\Delta G^\\ominus = \\Delta H^\\ominus - T\\Delta S^\\ominus$ = 40 000 - 500 × 100 = -10 000 J mol$^{-1}$.\nStep 2: $\\Delta G^\\ominus = -RT\\ln K$, so $\\ln K$ = 10 000 / (8.314 × 500) = 2.41.\nSo K = $e^{2.41}$ ≈ 11.1. Since $\\Delta G^\\ominus < 0$, K must exceed 1.\nThe value 0.090 comes from a sign error in $\\Delta G^\\ominus$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are accompanied by an increase in the entropy of the system?\n(i) A liquid crystallises into a solid\n(ii) A crystalline solid is heated from 0 K to 115 K\n(iii) $2NaHCO_3(s) \\rightarrow Na_2CO_3(s) + CO_2(g) + H_2O(g)$\n(iv) $H_2(g) \\rightarrow 2H(g)$\n(v) $2Cl(g) \\rightarrow Cl_2(g)$\n(vi) Two different gases diffuse into each other after the partition between them is removed","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Decrease: the solid formed is more ordered than the liquid.\n(ii) Increase: the particles begin to vibrate about their lattice positions, so disorder rises.\n(iii) Increase: one solid reactant gives one solid and two gaseous products.\n(iv) Increase: the number of gaseous particles doubles.\n(v) Decrease: two gaseous atoms combine into one molecule.\n(vi) Increase: the mixed gases are more random and less predictable.\nThe increases are (ii), (iii), (iv) and (vi), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A mixture of hydrogen and oxygen can be kept at room temperature for years without any noticeable formation of water. According to NCERT, the formation of water from this mixture is still called a spontaneous reaction. Which statement best explains why?","image":"","options":["Spontaneity means the process has the potential to proceed without help from an external agency; it says nothing about the rate","A spontaneous reaction must begin as soon as the reactants come into contact","The reaction is spontaneous only because it is endothermic","Any reaction that can be reversed on its own is spontaneous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key idea: a spontaneous process has the potential to proceed without the help of an external agency.\nSpontaneity does not tell us how fast the process occurs. The H$_2$–O$_2$ reaction is extremely slow at room temperature, but it is still spontaneous.\nOption (2) is the common misconception that spontaneous means instantaneous.\nOption (4) fails because a spontaneous process is irreversible and cannot reverse its direction on its own.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Molar volume of a gas is an intensive property even though volume is an extensive property.\nReason (R): The ratio of two extensive properties of a system is independent of the amount of matter, and hence is intensive.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Molar volume is $V_m = \\frac{V}{n}$. If the sample is doubled, both $V$ and $n$ double, so $V_m$ stays the same.\nR is true. When two quantities each scale with the amount of matter, their ratio does not depend on the amount.\nR also explains A, because $V$ and $n$ are both extensive. Density ($\\frac{m}{V}$) is another example of the same idea.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (the enthalpy change it represents).\nList-I:\n(a) CH$_4$(g) → C(g) + 4H(g)\n(b) C$_4$H$_{10}$(g) + $\\frac{13}{2}$O$_2$(g) → 4CO$_2$(g) + 5H$_2$O(l)\n(c) Na(s) → Na(g)\n(d) $\\frac{1}{2}$H$_2$(g) + $\\frac{1}{2}$Br$_2$(l) → HBr(g)\nList-II:\n(i) Standard enthalpy of formation\n(ii) Enthalpy of sublimation\n(iii) Standard enthalpy of combustion\n(iv) Four times the mean C–H bond enthalpy","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Atomization of CH$_4$: 1665 kJ mol$^{-1}$ = 4 × 416, i.e. four times the mean C–H bond enthalpy → (iv).\n(b) One mole of butane burnt completely → combustion (iii).\n(c) Solid directly to vapour → sublimation (ii).\n(d) One mole of HBr formed from its elements in their reference states → formation (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Given at 298 K:\nC(graphite, s) + O$_2$(g) → CO$_2$(g); $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ = −393.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nC(graphite, s) + $\\frac{1}{2}$O$_2$(g) → CO(g); $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ = −110.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nWhat is the standard enthalpy of combustion of CO(g), i.e., for CO(g) + $\\frac{1}{2}$O$_2$(g) → CO$_2$(g)?","image":"","options":["−283.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−504.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+283.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−172.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: eq (1) $\\Delta H_1$ = −393.5 and eq (2) $\\Delta H_2$ = −110.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula (Hess's law): target reaction = eq (1) − eq (2). Reversing eq (2) changes the sign of its enthalpy.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H$ = −393.5 − (−110.5) = −393.5 + 110.5.\nSo $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$(CO) = −283.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nOption (2) comes from adding the two values instead of subtracting.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For an isolated system $\\Delta U = 0$, so the energy change alone cannot tell whether a change in such a system is spontaneous; the entropy change can.\nStatement II: According to the third law of thermodynamics, the entropy of pure crystalline solids, solutions and supercooled liquids all become zero at 0 K.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In an isolated system the energy stays constant ($\\Delta U = 0$). A spontaneous change there shows up as $\\Delta S > 0$, so entropy, not energy, identifies the direction.\nStatement II is incorrect. The third law applies only to pure crystalline substances. The entropy of solutions and supercooled liquids is not zero at 0 K.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"1 mol of an ideal gas at 300 K, initially at 4 atm, is expanded isothermally to a final pressure of 1 atm in two steps. First it expands against a constant $p_{ex}$ of 2 atm until its pressure becomes 2 atm. Then it expands against a constant $p_{ex}$ of 1 atm until its pressure becomes 1 atm. What is the total work done on the gas? (R = 0.0821 L atm mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$-24.63$ L atm","$-18.47$ L atm","$-34.14$ L atm","$+24.63$ L atm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Volumes from $V = RT/p$ (RT = 24.63 L atm): 6.158 L at 4 atm, 12.315 L at 2 atm, 24.63 L at 1 atm.\nStep 1: $w_1 = -2(12.315 - 6.158) = -12.315$ L atm.\nStep 2: $w_2 = -1(24.63 - 12.315) = -12.315$ L atm.\nTotal: $w = -\\Sigma p\\Delta V = -24.63$ L atm.\nThe single-step value is $-18.47$ L atm and the reversible value is $-34.14$ L atm, so the two-step work lies between them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a certain decomposition reaction, $\\Delta_r H = +180\\ \\text{kJ mol}^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S = +150\\ \\text{J K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}$. Assume both are independent of temperature. Above what temperature does the reaction become spontaneous?","image":"","options":["1.2 K","120 K","1200 K","27 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H = 180000$ J mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta S = 150$ J K$^{-1}$mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: at the crossover point $\\Delta G = 0$, so $T = \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S}$.\nSubstituting: $T = \\frac{180000}{150} = 1200$ K.\nAbove 1200 K, $T\\Delta S > \\Delta H$, so $\\Delta G < 0$ and the reaction is spontaneous.\nOption (1) comes from forgetting to convert kJ to J.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For an ionic solid MX, the lattice enthalpy is +705 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The enthalpy of hydration of its gaseous ions is −717 kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the enthalpy of solution of MX in water?","image":"","options":["−12 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+12 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+1422 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−1422 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_{lattice}H$ = +705 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_{hyd}H$ = −717 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta_{sol}H = \\Delta_{lattice}H + \\Delta_{hyd}H$.\nSubstituting: 705 + (−717) = −12 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nSo dissolution of MX is slightly exothermic. Compare NaCl, where 788 − 784 = +4 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In Joule's experiments, water in an adiabatic container (initial state A, temperature $T_A$) was brought to a new state B in two ways. In one, 1 kJ of mechanical work was done by churning the water with paddles. In the other, 1 kJ of electrical work was done using an immersion rod. Which conclusion follows from these experiments?","image":"","options":["Mechanical work raises the temperature more than an equal amount of electrical work","The same adiabatic work produces the same change of state, so $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$ and U is a state function","Heat flows from the immersion rod into the water, so $\\Delta U = q$ in the second method","The internal energy of the water stays constant because the container is adiabatic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both methods gave the same temperature rise, $T_B - T_A$, for the same 1 kJ of work.\nSo a given amount of adiabatic work produces the same change of state, whatever the path. This lets us define U with $\\Delta U = U_2 - U_1 = w_{ad}$, which makes U a state function.\nOption (4) fails because adiabatic walls only stop heat transfer. Work done on the system still raises U.\nOption (3) fails because no heat crosses the adiabatic boundary. The electrical energy enters as work.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process of an ideal gas) with List-II (relation that applies).\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal reversible expansion\n(B) Isothermal irreversible expansion against constant $p_{ex}$\n(C) Adiabatic change\n(D) Expansion into vacuum\nList-II:\n(I) $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$\n(II) $w = 0$\n(III) $q = 2.303\\,nRT\\log\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$\n(IV) $q = p_{ex}(V_f - V_i)$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Isothermal reversible: $\\Delta U = 0$, so $q = -w = 2.303nRT\\log(V_f/V_i)$, giving III.\n(B) Isothermal irreversible: $q = -w = p_{ex}(V_f - V_i)$, giving IV.\n(C) Adiabatic: $q = 0$, so $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$, giving I.\n(D) Vacuum: $p_{ex} = 0$, so $w = 0$, giving II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an intensive property?","image":"","options":["Heat capacity","Enthalpy","Molar volume","Internal energy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Extensive properties depend on the amount of matter. Heat capacity, enthalpy and internal energy are all extensive.\nA molar property is an extensive property divided by the number of moles, $\\chi_m = \\frac{\\chi}{n}$. It does not depend on the amount of matter, so it is intensive.\nMolar volume $V_m = \\frac{V}{n}$ is therefore intensive.\nHeat capacity is tempting, but $C$ is directly proportional to the amount of substance. Only the molar heat capacity $C_m$ is intensive.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are extensive properties?\nmass, volume, internal energy, temperature, density, pressure, heat capacity, enthalpy, molar volume","image":"","options":["6","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Extensive properties depend on the amount of matter present:\n- Mass, volume, internal energy, heat capacity and enthalpy are extensive. That makes 5.\n- Temperature, density and pressure are intensive.\n- Molar volume is $\\frac{V}{n}$, the volume per mole. It does not depend on sample size, so it is intensive.\nTotal extensive properties = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A sample is burnt in a bomb calorimeter whose heat capacity is 15.0 kJ $K^{-1}$. The temperature of the calorimeter rises by 1.60 K. What is $\\Delta U$ for the combustion of this sample?","image":"","options":["$-24.0$ kJ","$+24.0$ kJ","$-9.38$ kJ","$-0.107$ kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $C_V = 15.0$ kJ $K^{-1}$ and $\\Delta T = 1.60$ K.\nFormula: heat gained by the calorimeter is $C_V\\Delta T$. The reaction loses the same amount of heat, so $q = -C_V\\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: $q = -15.0 \\times 1.60 = -24.0$ kJ.\nAt constant volume $\\Delta U = q_V$, so $\\Delta U = -24.0$ kJ. The negative sign shows the reaction is exothermic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a reaction at a given temperature, the standard Gibbs energy change $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ is negative. What is the value of its equilibrium constant K?","image":"","options":["Greater than 1","Less than 1","Exactly equal to 1","Equal to zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key relation: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -2.303RT\\log K$.\nIf $\\Delta_rG^\\circ < 0$, then $\\log K > 0$, so K > 1. The products are favoured at equilibrium.\nK = 1 would need $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = 0$.\nK < 1 would need $\\Delta_rG^\\circ > 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes have a positive enthalpy change?\n(i) Melting of ice at 273 K\n(ii) Sublimation of dry ice at 195 K\n(iii) Freezing of water at 273 K\n(iv) Vaporisation of water at 373 K\n(v) Condensation of steam at 373 K","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Positive: melting (fusion) absorbs heat, $\\Delta_{fus}H$ = +6.00 kJ/mol.\n(ii) Positive: sublimation absorbs heat, $\\Delta_{sub}H$ = +25.2 kJ/mol.\n(iii) Negative: freezing is the reverse of fusion, so heat is given off.\n(iv) Positive: vaporisation absorbs heat, +40.79 kJ/mol.\n(v) Negative: condensation is the reverse of vaporisation.\nThree processes have positive ΔH.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $CaCO_3(s) \\rightarrow CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$, $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus = +178.3$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S^\\ominus = +160$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Assuming both values do not change with temperature, above what temperature does the decomposition become spontaneous?","image":"","options":["1114 K","1.11 K","841 K","298 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H = +178300$ J mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta S = +160$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Both are positive, so the reaction is spontaneous only at high temperature.\nFormula: $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0$ requires $T > \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S}$.\nSubstituting: $T > \\frac{178300}{160} = 1114$ K.\nThe option 1.11 K comes from failing to convert kJ to J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The molar heat capacity at constant volume of a monoatomic ideal gas is 12.47 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. What is its molar heat capacity at constant pressure? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["20.78 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","4.16 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","16.63 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","29.10 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $C_V$ = 12.47 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: for an ideal gas, $C_p - C_V = R$.\nSubstituting: $C_p$ = 12.47 + 8.314 = 20.784.\nSo $C_p$ ≈ 20.78 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. Subtracting R instead of adding it would give the wrong value 4.16.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The enthalpy of atomization of ammonia, $NH_3(g) \\rightarrow N(g) + 3H(g)$, is 1170 kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the mean N–H bond enthalpy in ammonia?","image":"","options":["390 kJ mol$^{-1}$","1170 kJ mol$^{-1}$","585 kJ mol$^{-1}$","292.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_aH$ of $NH_3$ = 1170 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Atomization breaks all three N–H bonds.\nFormula: mean bond enthalpy = $\\Delta_aH$ / (number of identical bonds broken).\nSubstituting: 1170 / 3 = 390 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThis is the same method NCERT uses for $CH_4$: 1665/4 ≈ 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nDividing by 2 or 4 uses the wrong number of bonds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a state function?","image":"","options":["Internal energy, $U$","Enthalpy, $H$","Temperature, $T$","Heat, $q$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A state function depends only on the present state of the system, not on how that state was reached.\n$U$, $H$ and $T$ (like $p$ and $V$) are state functions.\nThe heat $q$ exchanged during a change depends on how the change is carried out, so it is a path function.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Using the standard enthalpies of formation at 298 K: $CH_4(g)$ = –74.81, $CO_2(g)$ = –393.5 and $H_2O(l)$ = –285.83 kJ $mol^{-1}$, calculate the heat released when 4.0 g of methane is completely burnt to $CO_2(g)$ and $H_2O(l)$ under standard conditions. (C = 12, H = 1)","image":"","options":["222.6 kJ","241.3 kJ","151.1 kJ","260.0 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $CH_4(g) + 2O_2(g) \\rightarrow CO_2(g) + 2H_2O(l)$. $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus(O_2)$ = 0.\nFormula: $\\Delta_c H^\\ominus = [-393.5 + 2(-285.83)] - [-74.81]$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_c H^\\ominus$ = –965.16 + 74.81 = –890.35 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nMoles of $CH_4$ = 4.0/16 = 0.25 mol, so heat released = 0.25 × 890.35 = 222.6 kJ.\nIgnoring the $CH_4$ term would give 241.3 kJ, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about thermochemical equations are correct?\n(i) The coefficients in a balanced thermochemical equation refer to moles, never to molecules.\n(ii) If all coefficients are halved, the value of $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ is also halved.\n(iii) When the equation is reversed, $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ keeps its magnitude but changes sign.\n(iv) The physical states of substances need not be mentioned, since $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ does not depend on them.\n(v) The standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its reference state is zero.\n(vi) Since freezing releases heat, some enthalpies of fusion are negative.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: coefficients refer to moles, never molecules.\n(ii) Correct: enthalpy is extensive, so halving the equation halves $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ (e.g. –33.3 becomes –16.6 kJ $mol^{-1}$ for $Fe_2O_3$ reduction).\n(iii) Correct: for example, –91.8 becomes +91.8 kJ $mol^{-1}$ for $NH_3$.\n(iv) Incorrect: physical and allotropic states must be specified because they change $\\Delta H$.\n(v) Correct: this is a standard convention.\n(vi) Incorrect: melting is endothermic, so every $\\Delta_{fus}H$ is positive.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the gas-phase reaction A(g) + 3B(g) → 2C(g) at 300 K, $\\Delta U = -20.0$ kJ and $\\Delta S = -60.0$ J K$^{-1}$. Calculate $\\Delta G$ for the reaction. ($R = 8.314$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["−6.99 kJ","−2.00 kJ","+2.99 kJ","−38.0 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta n_g = 2 - 4 = -2$, T = 300 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta H = \\Delta U + \\Delta n_gRT$, then $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H = -20000 + (-2)(8.314)(300) = -24988$ J.\n$T\\Delta S = 300 \\times (-60) = -18000$ J.\n$\\Delta G = -24988 + 18000 = -6988$ J.\nUsing $\\Delta U$ in place of $\\Delta H$ gives the wrong value −2.00 kJ.\nSo $\\Delta G$ ≈ −6.99 kJ, and the reaction is spontaneous.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Reactants are placed inside a thermos flask, and the flask together with its contents is taken as the system. What type of system is this?","image":"","options":["Open system","Isolated system","Closed system","Adiabatic open system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives reactants in a thermos flask, or any closed insulated vessel, as the example of an isolated system.\nThe insulated, sealed vessel allows neither matter nor energy to cross the boundary.\nA closed system is the tempting wrong choice. A closed system blocks matter but still exchanges energy, as a closed copper vessel does.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The enthalpy change of $CaO(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s)$ is not the standard enthalpy of formation of $CaCO_3(s)$","For $H_2(g) + Br_2(l) \\rightarrow 2HBr(g)$, $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ equals the standard enthalpy of formation of HBr(g)","When a thermochemical equation is reversed, $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ keeps the same sign but changes in magnitude","Coefficients in a thermochemical equation refer to the number of molecules of reactants and products"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct. Here $CaCO_3$ is formed from compounds, not from its constituent elements, so this $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ is not a formation enthalpy.\nOption (2) is wrong. Two moles of HBr form, so $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus = 2\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ of HBr.\nOption (3) is wrong. Reversing an equation reverses the sign of $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus$ and keeps its magnitude.\nOption (4) is wrong. The coefficients refer to moles, never molecules.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The standard enthalpy change for $H_2(g) + Br_2(l) \\rightarrow 2HBr(g)$ is not the standard enthalpy of formation of HBr(g).\nReason (R): $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ refers to the formation of exactly one mole of a compound from its constituent elements in their reference states.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The reaction forms 2 mol of HBr, so $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus = 2\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$(HBr, g). Halving the equation gives the formation reaction, with $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ = –36.4 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nR is true. By definition, $\\Delta_f H^\\ominus$ is for one mole of compound formed from its elements in their most stable states.\nThe reactants here are already elements in their reference states, so the only reason A holds is the two moles of product. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 50 g of water from 25°C to 45°C? Take the specific heat of water as 4.18 J g$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["4.18 kJ","2.09 kJ","8.36 kJ","0.209 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 50 g, c = 4.18 J g$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\Delta T$ = 45 − 25 = 20 K.\nFormula: $q = mc\\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: q = 50 × 4.18 × 20 = 4180 J.\nSo q = 4.18 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane is 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$, but the enthalpy needed to break the first C–H bond in $CH_4(g)$ is 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nReason (R): The four C–H bonds in methane differ from one another in bond length.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The mean value is $\\frac{1}{4}\\Delta_aH = \\frac{1665}{4} \\approx 416$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. The successive steps need 427, 439, 452 and 347 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nR is false. All four C–H bonds in $CH_4$ are identical in length and energy.\nThe successive values differ because each step breaks a bond in a different fragment ($CH_4$, $CH_3$, $CH_2$, $CH$), not because the bonds are different.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following best describes a reversible process in the thermodynamic sense?","image":"","options":["A process carried out in a single step against a large constant external pressure","A process that proceeds infinitely slowly through a series of equilibrium states and can be reversed at any moment by an infinitesimal change","Any process in which the system returns to its initial state after some time","A process in which no heat is exchanged between the system and the surroundings"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT definition: a reversible process can be reversed at any moment by an infinitesimal change. It proceeds infinitely slowly through equilibrium states, with $p_{ex} = p_{in} \\pm dp$.\nOption (1) describes a single-step irreversible process.\nOption (4) defines an adiabatic process, not a reversible one.\nOption (3) describes a cyclic change, which is not the same thing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the process Na(s) → Na(g), the standard enthalpy of atomization, $\\Delta_a H^\\circ$ = 108.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$. For this particular process, the enthalpy of atomization is the same as the","image":"","options":["standard enthalpy of sublimation of sodium","ionization enthalpy of sodium","bond dissociation enthalpy of the Na–Na bond in Na$_2$(g)","lattice enthalpy of sodium metal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Atomization produces gaseous atoms. Here solid sodium changes directly into gaseous Na atoms.\nA direct solid → vapour change is sublimation, so $\\Delta_a H^\\circ = \\Delta_{sub} H^\\circ$ = 108.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (NCERT).\nIonization enthalpy is wrong because it removes an electron from Na(g) and no electron is removed here.\nBond dissociation enthalpy is wrong because no gaseous diatomic molecule is broken.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The enthalpy of atomization of CH$_4$(g) into C(g) and 4H(g) is 1665 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The bond dissociation enthalpies for the first three successive steps are:\nCH$_4$ → CH$_3$ + H: 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nCH$_3$ → CH$_2$ + H: 439 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nCH$_2$ → CH + H: 452 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nWhat is the enthalpy change for CH(g) → C(g) + H(g)?","image":"","options":["347 kJ mol$^{-1}$","416 kJ mol$^{-1}$","452 kJ mol$^{-1}$","405 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_a H$ = 1665 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and three stepwise values.\nFormula (Hess's law): the sum of the four steps equals $\\Delta_a H$.\nSubstituting: fourth step = 1665 − (427 + 439 + 452) = 1665 − 1318.\nSo the fourth step = 347 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThe value 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$ is the mean C–H bond enthalpy (1665/4), not this step.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A system absorbs 500 J of heat from the surroundings and does 200 J of work on the surroundings. Using the IUPAC sign convention, what is the change in internal energy of the system?","image":"","options":["+700 J","+300 J","−300 J","−700 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: heat absorbed, so q = +500 J. Work is done by the system, so w = −200 J.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = q + w$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U = 500 + (-200)$ J\nSo $\\Delta U$ = +300 J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Reactants placed in a closed vessel made of copper form a closed system.\nStatement II: In an isolated system, energy can be exchanged with the surroundings but matter cannot.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A closed copper vessel lets heat pass through its conducting walls, but no matter can enter or leave, so it is a closed system.\nStatement II is incorrect. An isolated system, such as a thermos flask, exchanges neither energy nor matter with the surroundings. The description in Statement II (energy exchanged, matter not) actually defines a closed system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The enthalpy of formation of CO(g) cannot be measured directly by burning graphite in a limited supply of oxygen.\nReason (R): When graphite is burnt to form CO, some CO$_2$ is always produced along with it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that $\\Delta_r H$ for C + ½O$_2$ → CO cannot be measured directly.\nR is true: although CO is the major product, some CO$_2$ always forms. The measured heat therefore belongs to a mixture of two reactions.\nR explains A. This is why the value $-110.5$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ is obtained indirectly by Hess's law from $-393.5$ and $-283.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A 2.00 mol sample of an ideal gas absorbs 2078.5 J of heat at constant pressure, and its temperature rises by 50.0 K. What is the change in internal energy, $\\Delta U$, of the gas? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["1247.1 J","2078.5 J","831.4 J","1662.8 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 2.00 mol, $q_p$ = 2078.5 J, $\\Delta T$ = 50.0 K.\nStep 1: $C_{p,m} = q_p/(n\\Delta T)$ = 2078.5/(2 × 50) = 20.785 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nStep 2: $C_{V,m} = C_{p,m} - R$ = 20.785 − 8.314 = 12.471 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nStep 3: $\\Delta U = nC_{V,m}\\Delta T$ = 2 × 12.471 × 50 = 1247.1 J.\nCheck: $\\Delta U = q_p - nR\\Delta T$ = 2078.5 − 831.4 = 1247.1 J. Taking 2078.5 J ignores the expansion work, and 831.4 J is the work term only.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): No work is done when an ideal gas expands isothermally into a vacuum.\nReason (R): During free expansion the external pressure opposing the expansion is zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Expansion into a vacuum is free expansion, and $w = -p_{ex}\\Delta V$.\nR is true. In a vacuum, $p_{ex} = 0$.\nR explains A, because $p_{ex} = 0$ gives $w = -0 \\times \\Delta V = 0$. This holds whether the process is reversible or irreversible.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to the third law of thermodynamics, the entropy approaches zero as the temperature approaches 0 K for which of the following?","image":"","options":["A supercooled liquid","A pure, perfectly crystalline substance","An aqueous solution of a salt","Any substance in its solid state"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The third law states that the entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as T approaches absolute zero, because a perfect crystal at 0 K is perfectly ordered.\nNCERT notes that solutions and supercooled liquids do not have zero entropy at 0 K, so options (1) and (3) fail.\nOption (4) is too broad: a solid that is impure or not perfectly crystalline keeps some residual disorder.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In a bomb calorimeter, no work is done during the reaction, even when gases are produced or consumed. The reason is that:","image":"","options":["the reaction is carried out at constant pressure of 1 atm","the steel bomb is sealed, so its volume does not change ($\\Delta V = 0$)","the water bath absorbs all the work as heat","gases formed in combustion behave ideally and do no work"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pressure–volume work is $w = -p_{ex}\\Delta V$.\nThe steel bomb is sealed and rigid, so $\\Delta V = 0$ and therefore $w = 0$.\nInside the bomb the pressure can change, so the process is not at constant pressure. Option (1) is wrong.\nIdeal behaviour of the gases does not remove the work term. Only $\\Delta V = 0$ does.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is an intensive property?","image":"","options":["Mass","Enthalpy","Density","Heat capacity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An intensive property does not depend on the amount of matter present. An extensive property does.\nMass, enthalpy and heat capacity all grow in proportion to the size of the sample, so they are extensive.\nDensity is mass divided by volume. Both scale together, so the ratio stays fixed.\nDensity is therefore intensive.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to the effect of temperature on spontaneity (Table 5.4 of NCERT), which combination of signs makes a reaction non-spontaneous at low temperature but spontaneous at high temperature?","image":"","options":["$\\Delta_r H < 0$, $\\Delta_r S > 0$","$\\Delta_r H > 0$, $\\Delta_r S > 0$","$\\Delta_r H < 0$, $\\Delta_r S < 0$","$\\Delta_r H > 0$, $\\Delta_r S < 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$.\nWith $\\Delta H > 0$ and $\\Delta S > 0$, $\\Delta G$ is positive at low T. At high T the term $T\\Delta S$ outweighs $\\Delta H$, so $\\Delta G$ becomes negative.\nOption (1): $\\Delta G$ is negative at every temperature.\nOption (3): the reaction is spontaneous at low T only, which is the reverse of what is asked.\nOption (4): $\\Delta G$ is positive at every temperature.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane is about 416 kJ mol$^{-1}$, even though the enthalpies needed to break the four C–H bonds one after another are all different.\nStatement II: The lattice enthalpy of NaCl is measured directly by experiment, which is why no Born–Haber cycle is needed for it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The successive dissociation enthalpies are 427, 439, 452 and 347 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The mean value is $\\frac{1}{4}\\Delta_a H = \\frac{1665}{4} \\approx 416$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nStatement II is incorrect. Lattice enthalpy cannot be determined directly by experiment. It is obtained indirectly from a Born–Haber cycle using Hess's law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How much heat must be supplied to melt 36 g of ice at 273 K and 1 bar? (Given: $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\circ$ of ice = 6.00 kJ $mol^{-1}$; molar mass of water = 18 g $mol^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["12.0 kJ","6.0 kJ","3.0 kJ","216 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass = 36 g, M = 18 g $mol^{-1}$, $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\circ$ = 6.00 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: q = n × $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\circ$.\nSubstituting: n = 36/18 = 2 mol, so q = 2 × 6.00.\nSo heat required = 12.0 kJ. Melting is endothermic, so this heat is absorbed at constant temperature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In the Gibbs equation $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$ for a system, which quantity represents the net energy available to do useful work?","image":"","options":["$\\Delta G$","$T\\Delta S$","$\\Delta H$","$\\Delta S$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT says $T\\Delta S_{sys}$ is energy that is not available to do useful work.\n$\\Delta H$ is the total enthalpy change, so it is not the answer.\nWhat remains, $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$, is the net energy available for useful work. This is why G is called 'free energy'.\nThe most tempting wrong option, $T\\Delta S$, is in fact the unavailable part.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"One mole of an ideal gas at 300 K expands isothermally into a vacuum (free expansion) from 10 L to 20 L. What is $\\Delta S_{total}$ for the system plus surroundings?\n(Use R = 8.314 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$ and ln 2 = 0.693.)","image":"","options":["0 J K$^{-1}$","+5.76 J K$^{-1}$","–5.76 J K$^{-1}$","+11.52 J K$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Free expansion: $p_{ex}$ = 0, so w = 0. Also q = 0, so no heat reaches the surroundings and $\\Delta S_{surr}$ = 0.\nEntropy is a state function, so we can find $\\Delta S_{sys}$ using a reversible isothermal path between the same two states:\n$\\Delta S_{sys} = \\frac{q_{rev}}{T} = nR \\ln\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$ = 1 × 8.314 × 0.693 = 5.76 J K$^{-1}$\nSo $\\Delta S_{total}$ = +5.76 J K$^{-1}$, even though $\\Delta U$ = 0. This shows that $\\Delta S$, not $\\Delta U$, identifies an irreversible process.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightarrow 2NH_3(g)$, take $\\Delta_r H^\\ominus = -92.4$ kJ $mol^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S^\\ominus = -198.3$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$. Assume both values do not change with temperature, and that the surroundings stay at the temperature of the system. What is the total entropy change, $\\Delta S_{total}$, at 500 K?","image":"","options":["$-13.5$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$","$+386.1$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$","$+184.8$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$","$+111.7$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H_{sys} = -92400$ J $mol^{-1}$, $\\Delta S_{sys} = -198.3$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$, T = 500 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta S_{surr} = -\\frac{\\Delta H_{sys}}{T}$, and $\\Delta S_{total} = \\Delta S_{sys} + \\Delta S_{surr}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta S_{surr} = \\frac{92400}{500} = +184.8$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$.\nThen $\\Delta S_{total} = 184.8 - 198.3 = -13.5$ J $K^{-1}$ $mol^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta S_{total}$ is negative and the reaction is not spontaneous at 500 K. The value +111.7 is what you get at 298 K, and +386.1 comes from adding the magnitudes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction CaCO$_3$(s) → CaO(s) + CO$_2$(g), $\\Delta_r H$ = +178.3 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S_{sys}$ = +160.6 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Assume both values do not change with temperature. Above what temperature does $\\Delta S_{total}$ become positive?","image":"","options":["1110 K","837 K","1.11 K","298 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H$ = 178300 J mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta S_{sys}$ = 160.6 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta S_{total} = \\Delta S_{sys} - \\frac{\\Delta H}{T}$. This is positive when $T > \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S_{sys}}$.\nSubstituting: T = $\\frac{178300}{160.6}$ ≈ 1110 K\nSo the decomposition is spontaneous only above about 1110 K. Option (3) comes from forgetting to convert kJ to J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A 200 g block of a metal has a heat capacity of 180 J K$^{-1}$. The molar mass of the metal is 50 g mol$^{-1}$. How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 3.0 mol of the same metal from 300 K to 320 K?","image":"","options":["10800 J","2700 J","3600 J","2025 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the 200 g block has $C = 180$ J K$^{-1}$, $M = 50$ g mol$^{-1}$, and the second sample has $n = 3$ mol with $\\Delta T = 20$ K.\nFirst find the moles in the block: $n_1 = \\frac{200}{50} = 4$ mol.\nHeat capacity is extensive, so the molar heat capacity is $C_m = \\frac{C}{n_1} = \\frac{180}{4} = 45$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $q = n\\,C_m\\,\\Delta T = 3 \\times 45 \\times 20$.\nSo $q$ = 2700 J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"One mole of steam at 100°C and 1 bar is converted into ice at 0°C. Assuming water vapour behaves as a perfect gas and the volume change of the condensed phases is negligible, calculate the internal energy change $\\Delta U$ for the overall process.\nGiven: $\\Delta_{vap}H^\\circ$ (373 K) = 40.79 kJ $mol^{-1}$; $\\Delta_{fus}H^\\circ$ (273 K) = 6.00 kJ $mol^{-1}$; specific heat of liquid water = 4.2 J $g^{-1}$ °$C^{-1}$; M = 18 g $mol^{-1}$; R = 8.314 J $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["–54.35 kJ $mol^{-1}$","–51.25 kJ $mol^{-1}$","–57.45 kJ $mol^{-1}$","–48.15 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1 (condensation at 373 K): $\\Delta H_1$ = –40.79 kJ and $\\Delta n_g$ = –1, so $\\Delta U_1 = \\Delta H_1 - \\Delta n_g RT$ = –40.79 + (8.314 × 373)/1000 = –37.69 kJ.\nStep 2 (liquid cooled from 100°C to 0°C): $\\Delta H_2$ = –18 × 4.2 × 100 J = –7.56 kJ. The volume change is negligible, so $\\Delta U_2$ ≈ –7.56 kJ.\nStep 3 (freezing): $\\Delta U_3$ ≈ $\\Delta H_3$ = –6.00 kJ.\nTotal: $\\Delta U$ = –37.69 – 7.56 – 6.00 = –51.25 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nOption (1) is $\\Delta H$, not $\\Delta U$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The following thermochemical data are known at 298 K:\n$HCl(g) + 10\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl.10\\,aq$; $\\Delta H = -69.01$ kJ/mol\n$HCl(g) + 40\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl.40\\,aq$; $\\Delta H = -72.79$ kJ/mol\n$HCl(g) + \\infty\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl.\\infty\\,aq$; $\\Delta H = -74.85$ kJ/mol\nWhat is the enthalpy of dilution for $HCl.10\\,aq + 30\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl.40\\,aq$?","image":"","options":["$-3.78$ kJ/mol","$-0.76$ kJ/mol","$+3.78$ kJ/mol","$-5.84$ kJ/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H$ (10 aq) = $-69.01$ kJ/mol and $\\Delta H$ (40 aq) = $-72.79$ kJ/mol.\nMethod (Hess's law): subtract the 10 aq equation from the 40 aq equation. This gives $HCl.10\\,aq + 30\\,aq \\rightarrow HCl.40\\,aq$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H = -72.79 - (-69.01) = -3.78$ kJ/mol.\nThe value $-0.76$ kJ/mol is the dilution from 25 aq to 40 aq, a different change. The value $-5.84$ kJ/mol is dilution to infinite dilution.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas is held in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston. It expands from 2.0 L to 7.0 L in a single step against a constant external pressure of 2.0 atm. What is the work done on the gas? (1 L atm = 101.3 J)","image":"","options":["$-1013$ J","$+1013$ J","$-10$ J","$-2026$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{ex}$ = 2.0 atm, $V_i$ = 2.0 L, $V_f$ = 7.0 L.\nFormula: $w = -p_{ex}(V_f - V_i)$\nSubstituting: $w = -2.0 \\times (7.0 - 2.0) = -10$ L atm, and $-10 \\times 101.3 = -1013$ J.\nThe sign is negative because the gas does work on the surroundings during expansion. The option $-10$ J forgets the L atm to J conversion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero","The entropy of a supercooled liquid becomes exactly zero at 0 K","A given quantity of heat added at a lower temperature causes greater randomness than when added at a higher temperature","For a system at equilibrium, the entropy is maximum and $\\Delta S = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is the third law of thermodynamics, since a pure crystal is perfectly ordered at 0 K.\n(2) Incorrect: the third law is confined to pure crystalline solids. Solutions and supercooled liquids have non-zero entropy at 0 K.\n(3) Correct: this is why $\\Delta S = q_{rev}/T$ is inversely proportional to T.\n(4) Correct: entropy rises to a maximum and stops changing at equilibrium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For useful thermodynamic calculations, the state of the surroundings must be completely specified along with that of the system.\nStatement II: To completely define the state of a system, it is not necessary to specify all its properties; once a certain minimum number of macroscopic properties are fixed, the others automatically have definite values.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. NCERT notes that the state of the surroundings can never be completely specified, and fortunately this is not necessary.\nStatement II is true. Only a certain number of properties can be varied independently, and that number depends on the nature of the system.\nOnce those properties are fixed (for example $p$, $T$ and $n$ for a gas), the remaining properties are determined.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Enthalpy, H, is a state function even though heat, q, in general depends on the path.\nStatement II: The heat absorbed by a system at constant pressure, $q_p$, is independent of the path because $q_p = \\Delta H$.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. H = U + pV, and U, p and V are all state functions, so H is also a state function. This is true even though q in general depends on the path.\nStatement II: Correct. At constant pressure, $q_p = H_2 - H_1 = \\Delta H$. Since $\\Delta H$ depends only on the initial and final states, $q_p$ is also path independent.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In Joule's experiments, water in an insulated container was taken from state A to state B in two ways. In one, 1 kJ of mechanical work was done by churning the water with paddles. In the other, 1 kJ of electrical work was done using an immersion rod. What did these experiments establish?","image":"","options":["Mechanical work raises the temperature more than an equal amount of electrical work","A given amount of adiabatic work produces the same change of state irrespective of how it is done","Heat and work are both state functions","The internal energy of water decreases when work is done on it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both methods supplied 1 kJ of work through adiabatic walls, and both gave the same temperature change $T_B - T_A$.\nSo the change of state depends only on the amount of adiabatic work, not on how the work is done.\nThis justifies defining internal energy U as a state function, with $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$.\nOption (1) contradicts the observation. Work itself is path dependent, so (3) is wrong. Work done on the system increases U, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Cooking gas is mostly butane, C$_4$H$_{10}$. Its standard enthalpy of combustion is $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ = −2658 kJ mol$^{-1}$. How much heat is released when 29 g of butane burns completely? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1)","image":"","options":["1329 kJ","2658 kJ","664.5 kJ","5316 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 29 g, M(C$_4$H$_{10}$) = 4(12) + 10(1) = 58 g mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta_c H^\\circ$ = −2658 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: heat released = n × |$\\Delta_c H^\\circ$|, where n = m/M.\nSubstituting: n = 29/58 = 0.5 mol, so heat = 0.5 × 2658.\nSo the heat released = 1329 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas expands isothermally into vacuum (free expansion). Which set of values correctly describes this process?","image":"","options":["$w < 0$, $q > 0$, $\\Delta U = 0$","$w = 0$, $q = 0$, $\\Delta U = 0$","$w = 0$, $q > 0$, $\\Delta U > 0$","$w < 0$, $q = 0$, $\\Delta U < 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Work: in free expansion $p_{ex} = 0$, so $w = -p_{ex}\\Delta V = 0$.\nHeat: Joule found experimentally that $q = 0$ for the free expansion of an ideal gas.\nInternal energy: $\\Delta U = q + w = 0$.\nOption (1) describes an isothermal expansion against a non-zero external pressure, not expansion into vacuum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct (IUPAC sign convention)?\n(i) For an isolated system, $\\Delta U = 0$.\n(ii) For a given change of state, q and w are each independent of the path followed.\n(iii) When heat is transferred from the system to the surroundings, q is negative.\n(iv) If no work is done and the change occurs through thermally conducting walls, $\\Delta U = q$.\n(v) Work done by the system on the surroundings is taken as positive.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: there is no heat or work exchange, so q = 0, w = 0 and $\\Delta U = 0$.\n(ii) Incorrect: q and w individually depend on the path. Only their sum, $q + w = \\Delta U$, is path-independent.\n(iii) Correct: heat lost by the system lowers U, so q < 0.\n(iv) Correct: with w = 0, $\\Delta U = q$.\n(v) Incorrect: IUPAC takes work done by the system as negative. Positive w was the older physics convention.\nCorrect statements: 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston expands from 2.0 L to 6.0 L against a constant external pressure of 2.0 atm. What is the work done on the gas (IUPAC sign convention)?","image":"","options":["$-8.0$ L atm","$+8.0$ L atm","$-4.0$ L atm","$-12.0$ L atm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{ex}$ = 2.0 atm, $V_i$ = 2.0 L, $V_f$ = 6.0 L.\nFormula: $w = -p_{ex}(V_f - V_i)$.\nSubstituting: $w = -2.0 \\times (6.0 - 2.0) = -8.0$ L atm.\nThe gas does work on the surroundings during expansion, so $w$ is negative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas expand isothermally and reversibly at 300 K from 5 L to 50 L. Calculate the work done on the gas. (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, log 10 = 1)","image":"","options":["$-11.49$ kJ","$+11.49$ kJ","$-4.99$ kJ","$-5.74$ kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 2 mol, T = 300 K, $V_f/V_i$ = 50/5 = 10.\nFormula: $w_{rev} = -2.303\\,nRT \\log\\frac{V_f}{V_i}$.\nSubstituting: $w = -2.303 \\times 2 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times 1 = -11488$ J.\nSo w = $-11.49$ kJ (negative because the gas does work while expanding).\nOption (4) uses n = 1; option (3) leaves out the factor 2.303.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The molar enthalpy of vaporisation of water is greater than that of acetone.\nReason (R): The magnitude of a phase-transition enthalpy depends on the strength of intermolecular interactions, and water molecules are held by hydrogen bonds that are stronger than the dipole–dipole interactions in acetone.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that less heat is needed to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than 1 mol of water.\nR is true. The enthalpy change in a phase transformation depends on the intermolecular forces that must be overcome. Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules tightly in the liquid, while acetone has only weaker dipole–dipole interactions.\nR directly explains why water needs more energy to vaporise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a bomb calorimeter are correct?\n(i) The steel bomb is immersed in a water bath to ensure that no heat is lost to the surroundings.\n(ii) The combustible substance is burnt in pure dioxygen supplied in the bomb.\n(iii) The temperature change is converted to $q_V$ using the known heat capacity of the calorimeter.\n(iv) The heat measured equals $\\Delta H$ for every reaction, whatever the gases involved.\n(v) The reaction inside the bomb takes place at a constant pressure of 1 atm.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the purpose of the water bath.\n(ii) Correct: combustion is carried out in pure $O_2$ inside the bomb.\n(iii) Correct: $q = C\\Delta T$ gives the heat absorbed by the calorimeter.\n(iv) Incorrect: the bomb gives $\\Delta U$. It equals $\\Delta H$ only when $\\Delta n_g = 0$.\n(v) Incorrect: the bomb works at constant volume, not constant pressure.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following correctly describes the standard state of solid iron at 500 K?","image":"","options":["Pure iron at 1 atm and 298 K","Pure iron at 1 bar and 500 K","Iron in its most stable form at 1 bar and 273 K","Pure iron at 1 bar, always referred to 298 K whatever the temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar.\nSo the standard state of solid iron at 500 K is pure iron at 1 bar, at that same temperature of 500 K.\nTemperature is not part of the definition. Data are usually tabulated at 298 K only for convenience.\nOptions with 1 atm or a fixed 273 K or 298 K therefore fail.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): We can state an absolute value for the volume of a system in a given state, but not for its internal energy.\nReason (R): Internal energy is not a state function, so only its change can be measured.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that volume can be given an unambiguous absolute value. For internal energy, only the change $\\Delta U$ can be measured.\nR is false. U is a state function: $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$ depends only on the initial and final states.\nThe absolute value of U cannot be found because U includes every form of energy in the system. Being a state function does not stop us knowing only its changes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about measuring heat with a calorimeter at constant (atmospheric) pressure is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The heat measured, $q_p$, equals the reaction enthalpy $\\Delta_r H$","For an exothermic reaction, $q_p$ and $\\Delta_r H$ are both negative","The measurement is generally made under atmospheric pressure","Since the volume is held fixed, no pressure–volume work can occur"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: at constant pressure, $\\Delta H = q_p$, which is called the heat or enthalpy of reaction.\n(2) Correct: the system loses heat in an exothermic reaction, so $q_p < 0$ and $\\Delta_r H < 0$.\n(3) Correct: such calorimeters work at atmospheric pressure.\n(4) Incorrect: here the pressure is constant, not the volume. Volume may change, so $p\\Delta V$ work can occur. That is exactly why $q_p \\ne \\Delta U$ in general.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Hess's law of constant heat summation follows directly from which property of enthalpy?","image":"","options":["Enthalpy is a state function, so $\\Delta H$ depends only on the initial and final states","Enthalpy is an intensive property, so it does not depend on the amount of substance","Enthalpy change equals the heat absorbed at constant volume","Enthalpy change depends on the number of steps through which a reaction is carried out"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Enthalpy is a state function, so $\\Delta H$ between reactants and products does not depend on the path.\nTherefore the enthalpy change is the same whether the reaction occurs in one step or in several steps. This is Hess's law.\nOption 2 is wrong because enthalpy is extensive.\nOption 3 is wrong because heat at constant volume equals $\\Delta U$, not $\\Delta H$.\nOption 4 contradicts the law itself.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$ at 298 K, $\\Delta_rH^\\ominus = -92.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_rS^\\ominus = -198$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. Assuming these values, what is $\\log K$ for the reaction at 298 K? (R = 8.314 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["13.48","5.85","16.19","-5.85"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H^\\ominus = -92400$ J mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta S^\\ominus = -198$ J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$, T = 298 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta G^\\ominus = \\Delta H^\\ominus - T\\Delta S^\\ominus$ and $\\log K = -\\frac{\\Delta G^\\ominus}{2.303RT}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta G^\\ominus = -92400 + 298(198) = -33396$ J mol$^{-1}$.\n$\\log K = \\frac{33396}{2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 298} = 5.85$.\n13.48 is ln K, not log K. 16.19 is obtained by ignoring the entropy term.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (relation that applies).\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum\n(B) Adiabatic process\n(C) Process at constant volume\n(D) Process at constant pressure\nList-II:\n(I) $\\Delta U = q_V$\n(II) $\\Delta H = q_p$\n(III) $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$\n(IV) $q = 0$, $w = 0$, $\\Delta U = 0$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Free expansion: $p_{ex} = 0$, so $w = 0$. Joule found $q = 0$, so $\\Delta U = 0$. A matches IV.\n(B) Adiabatic: $q = 0$, so $\\Delta U = w_{ad}$. B matches III.\n(C) Constant volume: $\\Delta V = 0$, so no p–V work is done and $\\Delta U = q_V$. C matches I.\n(D) Constant pressure: heat absorbed equals the enthalpy change, $\\Delta H = q_p$. D matches II.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas is taken between the same two volumes $V_i$ and $V_f$ at constant temperature in different ways.\nStatement I: For compression from $V_i$ to $V_f$, the work done on the gas in a single step against a constant $p_{ex}$ (equal to the final pressure) is greater than the work done on it in a reversible compression, where $p_{ex} = p_{in} + dp$ at every stage.\nStatement II: In a reversible process, the system and surroundings remain in near equilibrium throughout, and the process can be reversed at any moment by an infinitesimal change.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On a pV-plot, single-step compression covers a rectangle of height equal to the final pressure. Reversible compression covers only the area under the isotherm, which is smaller. So more work is done on the gas in the single-step process.\nStatement II is correct. This is the NCERT definition: a reversible process proceeds infinitely slowly through a series of equilibrium states.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightarrow 2NH_3(g)$ at 298 K, what is the value of $\\Delta H - \\Delta U$? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["−4.96 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+4.96 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−2.48 kJ mol$^{-1}$","−7.43 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: T = 298 K. Moles of gaseous products = 2 and moles of gaseous reactants = 1 + 3 = 4, so $\\Delta n_g$ = 2 − 4 = −2.\nFormula: $\\Delta H - \\Delta U = \\Delta n_g RT$.\nSubstituting: (−2)(8.314)(298) = −4955 J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta H - \\Delta U$ ≈ −4.96 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The positive value comes from taking $\\Delta n_g$ as reactants minus products.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-THERMODYNAMICS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction N$_2$(g) + 3H$_2$(g) → 2NH$_3$(g) at 298 K, for which $\\Delta_r H$ = –92.4 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_r S_{sys}$ = –198.3 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. What is the total entropy change, $\\Delta S_{total}$?","image":"","options":["+111.8 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$","–508.4 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$","+310.1 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$","–111.8 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta H$ = –92400 J mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta S_{sys}$ = –198.3 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$, T = 298 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta S_{surr} = -\\frac{\\Delta H}{T}$, and $\\Delta S_{total} = \\Delta S_{sys} + \\Delta S_{surr}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta S_{surr} = \\frac{92400}{298}$ = +310.1 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$\nSo $\\Delta S_{total}$ = –198.3 + 310.1 = +111.8 J K$^{-1}$ mol$^{-1}$. It is positive, so the reaction is spontaneous.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 5 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"}]});
